.docs/decisions/0047-entity-dashboard-architecture.md:1:# ADR-0047: Entity dashboard architecture — two-file pattern with bidirectional sync
.docs/decisions/0047-entity-dashboard-architecture.md:5:**Builds on:** [ADR-0004](0004-hybrid-json-markdown-architecture.md) (JSON + Markdown), [ADR-0018](0018-hybrid-storage-markdown-sqlite.md) (Markdown + SQLite), [ADR-0028](0028-structured-document-schemas.md) (structured document schemas), [ADR-0045](0045-entity-abstraction.md) (entity abstraction), [ADR-0046](0046-entity-mode-architecture.md) (entity-mode architecture)
.docs/decisions/0047-entity-dashboard-architecture.md:18:ADR-0004 established JSON for machines, markdown for humans. ADR-0018 established markdown as source of truth, SQLite as disposable cache. ADR-0028 established JSON-first schemas with markdown generated from JSON. These decisions are sound but create a tension for entity dashboards: who owns what when multiple tools can write?
.docs/decisions/0047-entity-dashboard-architecture.md:129:- After meeting capture (new wins/risks/decisions)
.docs/decisions/0047-entity-dashboard-architecture.md:173:6. If enrichment fails, the company overview section is empty — the dashboard still renders with all other data (fault-tolerant, per ADR-0042 pattern)
.docs/decisions/0047-entity-dashboard-architecture.md:212:- Chose JSON as canonical over markdown as canonical — avoids fragile markdown parsing (ADR-0004 rationale), accepts that external write path requires JSON knowledge
