.docs/decisions/0126-memory-substrate-invariants.md:1:# ADR-0126 — Memory Substrate Invariants
.docs/decisions/0126-memory-substrate-invariants.md:10:The v1.4.0 claim substrate (DOS-7 `intelligence_claims`, DOS-5 Trust Compiler, DOS-10 freshness decay, DOS-211 Provenance, DOS-212 DataSource taxonomy, DOS-213 prompt fingerprinting, DOS-263 invalidation_jobs, DOS-265 claim_edges, DOS-280 canonicalization) is large, interlocking, and built by multiple contributors over multiple releases. Without a unifying set of invariants, each contributor independently makes small decisions that drift apart over time. The result: two code paths that could in principle do the same thing end up doing subtly different things, ghost-resurrection bugs re-emerge after they were fixed, and every new feature ends up re-solving the "how do I store this?" problem.
.docs/decisions/0126-memory-substrate-invariants.md:12:A memory substrate that CSMs trust over many quarters needs a few structural guarantees that no single ticket enforces on its own. This ADR lists them so future tickets can point here instead of re-asserting them piecemeal.
.docs/decisions/0126-memory-substrate-invariants.md:14:The spirit is also aspirational: we looked at neuroscience's memory mechanisms (Hebbian strengthening, synaptic pruning, offline consolidation, schema-based assimilation, reconsolidation on retrieval) as a source of structural ideas. We adopt the mechanisms. We explicitly reject the failure modes the brain has because of its biological constraints — confabulation, irreversible forgetting, retrieval-induced distortion, schema-suppression of anomalies, one-winner contradiction resolution. Software has different constraints and can do better. This ADR locks down which side of that line the substrate sits on.
.docs/decisions/0126-memory-substrate-invariants.md:18:The memory substrate is governed by the following invariants. Each is enforced in code (CI lint or test) where possible. Each feature ticket either honors these invariants or proposes an amendment to this ADR.
.docs/decisions/0126-memory-substrate-invariants.md:38:`DELETE FROM intelligence_claims` is forbidden outside user-data-deletion flows (GDPR/CCPA). Same for `claim_corroborations` and `claim_contradictions`.
.docs/decisions/0126-memory-substrate-invariants.md:42:Enforcement: CI lint grep for `DELETE FROM intelligence_claims|claim_corroborations|claim_contradictions` outside the allowed paths.
.docs/decisions/0126-memory-substrate-invariants.md:48:Explicit user judgment (correction, tombstone, confirmation) flows through `claim_feedback` per ADR-0123 typed feedback semantics. Explicit judgment affects trust via DOS-5's `fb` factor.
.docs/decisions/0126-memory-substrate-invariants.md:54:When a new signal fits an existing claim schema (ADR-0125 claim type registry), it assimilates: strengthens corroborations, bumps freshness, does not produce a new surface notification.
.docs/decisions/0126-memory-substrate-invariants.md:66:Reconciliation happens via one of four explicit kinds (`user_picked_winner`, `evidence_converged`, `merged_as_qualified`, `both_dormant`). Auto-supersession for time-varying claim types (renewal_date, contract_end, ARR) records `reconciliation_kind='evidence_converged'` and uses ADR-0113's supersede path — no new supersession mechanism.
.docs/decisions/0126-memory-substrate-invariants.md:83:Budgets, if used, are per claim_type + sensitivity + surface. A critical-sensitivity health claim does not compete for the same attention slot as a routine adoption-metric. The claim type registry (ADR-0125) carries the per-type budget policy, not a global constant.
.docs/decisions/0126-memory-substrate-invariants.md:90:- Feedback events (correction, confirmation, tombstone) → `claim_feedback` (ADR-0123 typed) → DOS-5's `fb` factor → trust.
.docs/decisions/0126-memory-substrate-invariants.md:102:- **edge** — claim-derived relationship in the semantic graph (DOS-265), distinct from corroboration evidence
.docs/decisions/0126-memory-substrate-invariants.md:103:- **signal** — runtime event per ADR-0115 emitted on substrate writes
.docs/decisions/0126-memory-substrate-invariants.md:119:- **Hard-coded thresholds (`HOMEOSTATIC_BUDGET=100`, `DORMANT_THRESHOLD=0.15`, `STALE_WINDOW=180`).** Policy lives in the ADR-0125 claim type registry or role preset config (DOS-178), never magic numbers in code.
.docs/decisions/0126-memory-substrate-invariants.md:121:- **`user_dismissed_soft` as a surfacing demotion reason.** User intent is a tombstone (`claim_state='tombstoned'`), not a system surfacing decision.
.docs/decisions/0126-memory-substrate-invariants.md:128:- Review has a concrete list to check against: "does this ticket preserve the invariants in ADR-0126? If not, which one does it amend?"
.docs/decisions/0126-memory-substrate-invariants.md:133:- New features that would be easy to slam into a single column on `intelligence_claims` have to route through the right substrate layer. Some features will take slightly longer to ship as a result; the tradeoff is that the substrate doesn't decay over time.
.docs/decisions/0126-memory-substrate-invariants.md:135:**Who owns this ADR:**
.docs/decisions/0126-memory-substrate-invariants.md:138:- Claim types + registry: ADR-0125.
.docs/decisions/0126-memory-substrate-invariants.md:143:Amendments to this ADR happen when a feature ticket proposes a principled exception. Amendments are numbered and dated, not silently merged.
.docs/decisions/0126-memory-substrate-invariants.md:148:- DOS-7 (intelligence_claims — amendments A, B, C, D, E trace back here).
.docs/decisions/0126-memory-substrate-invariants.md:153:- Original framing: DailyOS Linear DOS-312 (closed; converted to this ADR).
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:1:# ADR-0106: Prompt Fingerprinting and Provider Interface Extension
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:6:**Extends:** [ADR-0091](0091-intelligence-provider-abstraction.md), [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md)  
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:7:**Depends on:** [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md)  
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:8:**Related:** [ADR-0110](0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md) (consumer of fingerprints)
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:12:[ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) §1 declares that every ability that invokes the intelligence provider records a `PromptFingerprint` in its provenance envelope. [ADR-0110](0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md) (forthcoming) uses the fingerprint to distinguish prompt regressions from input regressions during evaluation. [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md) §6 requires replay providers to produce deterministic completions in `Evaluate` mode.
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:14:[ADR-0091](0091-intelligence-provider-abstraction.md) defines `IntelligenceProvider::complete(prompt, tier) -> String`. This signature does not expose the metadata — provider name, model, token counts, seed, prompt template version — that `PromptFingerprint` requires. ADR-0091 was scoped to the intel queue; ADR-0102 expanded provider use to all Transform abilities. This ADR formally amends ADR-0091's trait to expose fingerprint-capable metadata and specifies the fingerprint shape and canonicalization rules.
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:36:**ModelName is a newtype, not `&'static str`.** Models are configurable at runtime per [ADR-0091](0091-intelligence-provider-abstraction.md); a static string would prevent user-selected models from being captured.
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:51:ADR-0091's trait is amended. The `complete()` method returns a `Completion` struct instead of `String`:
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:80:**Backward compatibility with ADR-0091.** Existing callers that receive only the text can do so via a thin helper `provider.complete(prompt, tier).await?.text`. The text field is unchanged. The added metadata is additive.
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:84:[ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md) §6 requires replay providers in `Evaluate` mode. This ADR specifies:
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:90:**Fixture construction.** Running an ability in `Live` mode with a tracing wrapper records every provider invocation (prompt template ID, inputs, metadata, completion). The captured sequence is the replay fixture for future `Evaluate` runs. [ADR-0110](0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md) specifies fixture storage, anonymization, and refresh cadence.
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:104:Prompt changes are independent of ability schema versions ([ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) §8). A prompt bump produces a new fingerprint without bumping the ability version. `prompt_template_version` in the fingerprint lets consumers distinguish prompt-driven output changes from schema-driven ones.
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:108:[ADR-0110](0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md) uses fingerprints for regression classification:
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:127:1. **`IntelligenceProvider` trait amendment is a breaking change for existing consumers.** ADR-0091's callers must migrate to the `Completion` return type. Helper `.text` field minimizes churn.
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:137:4. **Fixture bloat.** Every prompt+input combination recorded creates a replay entry. Mitigation: fixtures are scoped to specific evaluation tasks, not all traffic. [ADR-0110](0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md) details retention and pruning.
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:142:- [ADR-0091: IntelligenceProvider Abstraction](0091-intelligence-provider-abstraction.md) — Amended by this ADR: `complete()` returns `Completion` instead of `String`.
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:143:- [ADR-0102: Abilities as the Runtime Contract](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) — Prompt changes do not bump ability versions; version discipline in §8.
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:144:- [ADR-0104: ExecutionMode and Mode-Aware Services](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md) — §6 requires determinism in `Evaluate` mode, satisfied by replay keyed by `canonical_prompt_hash`.
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:145:- [ADR-0105: Provenance as First-Class Output](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) — §1 `PromptFingerprint` shape referenced; this ADR provides the definition.
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:146:- **ADR-0110 (forthcoming): Evaluation Harness for Abilities** — Consumes fingerprints for regression classification.
.docs/decisions/0036-inbox-processing-in-phase-1.md:1:# ADR-0036: Inbox processing implemented in Phase 1
