.docs/decisions/0087-entity-hierarchy-intelligence.md:1:# ADR-0087: Entity Hierarchy Intelligence — Portfolio Surfaces, Partner Type, Bidirectional Propagation
.docs/decisions/0087-entity-hierarchy-intelligence.md:5:**Extends:** ADR-0086 (Intelligence as Shared Service)
.docs/decisions/0087-entity-hierarchy-intelligence.md:7:**Extends:** ADR-0079 (Role Presets) — the `entityModeDefault` field in each preset determines which entity type receives the portfolio surface as primary. This ADR implements the intelligence architecture; ADR-0079 governs which entity type it applies to for a given role.
.docs/decisions/0087-entity-hierarchy-intelligence.md:74:Propagation loops are prevented by the existing mechanism: derived signals are not re-propagated in the same cycle.
.docs/decisions/0087-entity-hierarchy-intelligence.md:76:### 5. The portfolio model generalizes across entity types via ADR-0079
.docs/decisions/0087-entity-hierarchy-intelligence.md:86:The v0.13.3 implementation focuses on accounts (the dominant current use case). Project hierarchy intelligence follows in v0.13.4 using the identical architecture with project-appropriate vocabulary and prompt shape. People are explicitly excluded from this model — people don't have a tree hierarchy in DailyOS, they have a relationship network, which requires a different architectural approach not covered by this ADR.
.docs/decisions/0087-entity-hierarchy-intelligence.md:92:This is explicitly deferred. The expected user behavior is to tag meetings at the parent level rather than tagging every relevant child. The bidirectional propagation model (decision 4) partially addresses this: parent-tagged meetings produce parent-level signals that cascade down to children via fan-out. Content-level multi-entity extraction is a refinement, not a prerequisite.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:1:# ADR-0107: Source Taxonomy Alignment
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:6:**Extends:** [ADR-0098](0098-data-governance-source-aware-lifecycle.md), [ADR-0100](0100-glean-first-intelligence-architecture.md)  
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:7:**Supersedes:** The narrow `DataSource` enums implicit in [ADR-0098](0098-data-governance-source-aware-lifecycle.md) and [ADR-0100](0100-glean-first-intelligence-architecture.md); this ADR defines the authoritative taxonomy.  
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:8:**Required by:** [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) §4, [ADR-0108](0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md)
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:12:Three ADRs touch source taxonomy:
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:14:- [ADR-0098](0098-data-governance-source-aware-lifecycle.md) defines a `data_source` tag and purge-on-revocation semantics. Its implicit enum is `user, google, glean, clay, ai, co_attendance`.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:15:- [ADR-0100](0100-glean-first-intelligence-architecture.md) introduces `local_enrichment` and describes Glean as a multi-source feeder from Salesforce, Zendesk, Gong, Slack, P2, and org directories.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:16:- [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) §4 references `DataSource` in provenance attribution, including Salesforce and others not in ADR-0098's enum.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:18:The field naming is also inconsistent: [ADR-0080](0080-signal-intelligence-architecture.md) uses `source`; [ADR-0098](0098-data-governance-source-aware-lifecycle.md) and [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) use `data_source`. Lifecycle behavior per source (purge vs. flag-for-re-enrichment) is also not consistent: [ADR-0098](0098-data-governance-source-aware-lifecycle.md) says AI-generated intelligence is flagged for re-enrichment rather than purged, but downstream consumers (provenance, maintenance audit) had been assumed to purge.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:20:This ADR defines the authoritative `DataSource` enum, the `SourceIdentifier` extension pattern, the canonical field name, and the per-source lifecycle rules that every provenance-consuming surface honors.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:42:    Other(SourceName),       // Stable registered name; ADR amendment required to add
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:58:**Glean is modeled as an aggregator, not as a single source.** When an ability attributes `DataSource::Glean { downstream: Salesforce }`, it captures both the retrieval path (Glean) and the originating source (Salesforce). This preserves ADR-0100's Glean-first posture without erasing downstream lineage needed for trust and lifecycle decisions.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:60:**Adding a new source requires an ADR amendment.** The `Other(SourceName)` variant exists for forward compatibility but is not the authoring path. New integrations add explicit enum variants via an ADR, keeping the taxonomy discoverable and typed.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:64:Every place that stores or references a source of truth uses the field name `data_source`. This supersedes [ADR-0080](0080-signal-intelligence-architecture.md)'s `source` field on `SignalEvent`.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:72:`SourceIdentifier` from [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) §4 gains variants to match the expanded `DataSource` enum:
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:109:**Glean-sourced opaque data attribution.** When Glean returns an assessment derived from content DailyOS has no local visibility into (e.g., a Zendesk ticket the user's Glean account can read but DailyOS cannot), the ability attributes `DataSource::Glean { downstream: Zendesk }` with `SourceIdentifier::OpaqueGleanSource { downstream: Zendesk, opaque_ref, ... }`. Lifecycle of opaque sources is delegated to the user's Glean policy — DailyOS cannot purge data it does not hold, but it can mask or invalidate derived artifacts when the Glean connection is revoked.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:122:| `Glean { downstream: Slack }` | Context | Per [ADR-0100](0100-glean-first-intelligence-architecture.md) — context only |
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:123:| `Glean { downstream: P2 }` | Context | Per [ADR-0100](0100-glean-first-intelligence-architecture.md) — context only |
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:131:The `scoring_class` field on `SourceAttribution` ([ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) §4) is derived from this table, not author-set. Consumers that aggregate sources for scoring filter by class to avoid conflating Slack chatter with Salesforce ARR.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:135:[ADR-0098](0098-data-governance-source-aware-lifecycle.md)'s lifecycle rules are specified per source class:
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:142:    /// Mark the data as derived-from-revoked-source; surface as masked, do not delete.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:160:| `Glean { downstream }` | Mask (DailyOS cannot purge what it did not store; masks downstream-derived state) |
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:162:| `Ai` | FlagForReEnrichment (per ADR-0098) |
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:166:**Resolution of the ADR-0098/ADR-0105 conflict.** ADR-0098 is right: AI-synthesized output is flagged, not purged, because the underlying source revocation removes the input data but the synthesis itself may remain useful as an annotation awaiting re-generation. [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) §8 is amended: "source revocation triggers a masking pass" applies only to sources whose `LifecycleBehavior` is `Mask`; for `Purge`, the records are deleted; for `FlagForReEnrichment`, the records remain with a flag. [ADR-0108](0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md) specifies the masking and flag-rendering shapes surfaces see.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:170:Every stored field that is itself LLM-synthesized carries a `SynthesisMarker` (per [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) §4) so the trust model's `contains_stored_synthesis` check works. This ADR specifies the marker storage:
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:176:Per-ability migration plan is in [ADR-0112](0112-migration-strategy-parallel-run-and-cutover.md).
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:182:1. **One authoritative `DataSource` enum.** No drift between ADRs; every consumer uses the same values.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:186:5. **Lifecycle behavior per class.** ADR-0098's intent is now explicit: AI is flagged, Google is purged, Glean is masked.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:188:7. **Stored AI output is identifiable.** Synthesis markers close the trust-laundering hole from [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) §3.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:195:4. **`Other(SourceName)` escape hatch.** Tempting shortcut to skip ADR amendments for new integrations.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:200:2. **Opaque source purge expectations.** A user expects "revoke Glean" to purge all Glean-derived data; DailyOS can only mask because it cannot reach into Glean's stores. Mitigation: Settings UX explains masking behavior; Glean-side revocation is the user's responsibility via Glean's own controls.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:201:3. **Scoring class drift.** A future integration's scoring class is debated. Mitigation: default new integrations to `Reference` (most conservative) and require ADR amendment to promote to `Scoring` or `Context`.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:206:- [ADR-0098: Data Governance — Source-Aware Lifecycle](0098-data-governance-source-aware-lifecycle.md) — This ADR extends with formal enum, per-source lifecycle behavior, and resolves ADR-0098/ADR-0105 conflict on AI output (flagged, not purged).
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:207:- [ADR-0100: Glean-First Intelligence Architecture](0100-glean-first-intelligence-architecture.md) — This ADR extends with `GleanDownstream` enum preserving downstream attribution; honors context-only rule for Slack/P2.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:208:- [ADR-0080: Signal Intelligence Architecture](0080-signal-intelligence-architecture.md) — `source` field renamed to `data_source` via schema migration.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:209:- [ADR-0105: Provenance as First-Class Output](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) — §4 `SourceAttribution` uses this ADR's `DataSource` and `SourceIdentifier`.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:210:- [ADR-0108: Provenance Rendering and Privacy](0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md) — Renders masking behavior and flag-for-re-enrichment status.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:211:- [ADR-0112: Migration Strategy — Parallel Run and Cutover](0112-migration-strategy-parallel-run-and-cutover.md) — Specifies the `source` → `data_source` migration and the synthesis-marker backfill.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:219:Codex round 2 finding 13 surfaced that the v1.4.0 spine plan (DOS-299) needed to preserve item-level confidence semantics for legacy items without `ItemSource` (the current `HasSource::effective_confidence` defaults missing source to `0.5` per `intelligence/io.rs:1185-1189`). My earlier writeup proposed registering this in the `CLAIM_TYPE_REGISTRY` (per ADR-0125). That was wrong — it's a `DataSource` taxonomy concern, not a claim type. ADR-0107 owns `DataSource`; this amendment adds the variant.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:238:- **`ScoringClass`:** `Reference` (most conservative — these claims cannot drive scoring decisions on their own).
.docs/decisions/0012-profile-indicator-in-sidebar.md:1:# ADR-0012: Profile indicator in sidebar header
.docs/decisions/0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md:1:# ADR-0117: The Publish Boundary — Pencil and Pen
.docs/decisions/0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md:6:**Extends:** [ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md), [ADR-0103](0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md)
.docs/decisions/0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md:7:**Related:** [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md), [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md), [ADR-0108](0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md), [ADR-0111](0111-surface-independent-ability-invocation.md)
.docs/decisions/0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md:11:[ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) §3 introduces four ability categories: Read, Transform, Publish, and Maintenance. Three of them write to state that DailyOS controls — in-memory, in the local DB, or in an outbox under [ADR-0103](0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md). A failed Maintenance ability can be retried, rolled back, or compensated. A failed Read or Transform ability simply does not update anything.
.docs/decisions/0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md:15:This asymmetry — reversible inside DailyOS, irreversible once the side effect lands — is architecturally distinct. It requires a different contract from the user and a different implementation discipline from the authoring ability. We have one Publish ability today (`publish_to_p2`), referenced in [ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) §3 and [ADR-0112](0112-migration-strategy-parallel-run-and-cutover.md) §3. Before new Publish abilities land — and before `publish_to_p2` itself gets wrapped in the v1.4.0 abilities runtime — the class needs a shared protocol.
.docs/decisions/0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md:17:The metaphor this ADR formalizes: **Pencil and Pen.** Every publish is a two-phase operation. Pencil is the draft — reviewable, edit-in-place, infinitely reversible, lives in DailyOS. Pen is the send — irreversible side effect, user-confirmed, materializes outside DailyOS. The transition from Pencil to Pen is the only asymmetric step, and it requires an explicit user or policy gate.
.docs/decisions/0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md:19:This ADR specifies the protocol, the idempotency guarantees, the retraction behavior where applicable, the outbox integration, and the surface requirements for all Publish abilities present and future.
.docs/decisions/0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md:36:    pub provenance: Provenance,
.docs/decisions/0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md:46:- A Draft is a row in `publish_drafts`. It is DailyOS-internal, encrypted like any other table, subject to [ADR-0116](0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md) — content never leaves the device during the Pencil phase.
.docs/decisions/0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md:84:Publish commits go through the [ADR-0103](0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md) outbox pattern. The outbox is the boundary between "we intend to publish" and "we have published."
.docs/decisions/0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md:103:- `commit_publish` writes the outbox entry and returns. The actual delivery happens via a worker pool, identical in shape to the invalidation job worker ([ADR-0115](0115-signal-granularity-audit.md) §5).
.docs/decisions/0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md:154:`DestinationClient` implementations are mode-aware ([ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md)): under `Evaluate`, they route to a replay layer that records expected calls and returns canned responses. No real external calls in test mode, ever.
.docs/decisions/0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md:163:- The **provenance summary** — the top-level sources feeding the content, consistent with [ADR-0108](0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md) rendering rules.
.docs/decisions/0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md:170:Under `Evaluate` ([ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md)):
.docs/decisions/0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md:177:This means evaluation harness fixtures ([ADR-0110](0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md)) can end-to-end exercise a publish path without sending anything externally. Regression tests for Publish abilities are as cheap as any other ability.
.docs/decisions/0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md:184:- The `publish_outbox` row records the commit decision, the confirmation token, the delivery attempts, the destination ref, and the final status.
.docs/decisions/0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md:185:- The Provenance envelope ([ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md)) on the `AbilityOutput<PublishedRecord>` returned by the Publish ability includes a `publish_context` field with the outbox entry ID and the destination ref, enabling anyone reading the output later to trace exactly what went out and where.
.docs/decisions/0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md:191:Only abilities registered with `may_publish = true` in the ability registry may call `commit_publish`. Maintenance abilities, which can mutate internal state freely under [ADR-0103](0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md), may not publish unless their registry entry explicitly grants publish authority. Granting `may_publish` requires an ADR amendment — it is not a routine configuration change.
.docs/decisions/0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md:200:- Outbox worker integrated with the invalidation worker pool from [ADR-0115](0115-signal-granularity-audit.md) §5.
.docs/decisions/0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md:209:- Additional destination clients (Slack, email, webhook) — future work per destination-specific ADRs.
.docs/decisions/0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md:225:- **Two new tables and a worker pool add operational surface.** Mitigated by reusing the [ADR-0115](0115-signal-granularity-audit.md) §5 worker infrastructure; minimal net new code for the queue substrate.
.docs/decisions/0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md:226:- **Pencil/Pen adds a click to existing publish flows.** Accepted — the click is the product. Surfaces may combine "Preview + Send" into a single panel to keep the user experience tight, but the decision to send must be a distinct gesture.
.docs/decisions/0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md:235:- The outbox infrastructure is identical in shape to [ADR-0115](0115-signal-granularity-audit.md) — workers, retry, dead-letter — and reuses that code.
.docs/decisions/0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md:241:Founder decision (D2, recorded in [ADR-0116](0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md) "Founder commitment" section) makes the publish framework strategically load-bearing for DailyOS's enterprise commercial story.
.docs/decisions/0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md:243:The reasoning: enterprise buyers will ask for "leadership visibility into team activity." The architectural answer is not to soften [ADR-0116](0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md)'s metadata-only control-plane boundary. The architectural answer is that **publish is the channel** — a user-initiated push (or user-scheduled push) to an enterprise storage destination the customer controls. DailyOS does not reach into the customer's storage; the user publishes to it. Enterprise gets visibility into what their users choose to publish, in the format (human- or machine-readable) the destination expects, on the cadence the user sets. The control plane sees zero of it.
.docs/decisions/0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md:247:**Scope correction — 2026-04-20 (outside voice finding #1):** Publish is **reporting/export**, not **team intelligence**. The earlier framing of this section implied publish was the complete answer to "enterprise wants leadership views." It isn't. Publish solves: "user creates a snapshot of their work, pushes it to a customer-controlled destination on a cadence they set." That is reporting. It does NOT solve: "a team sees the same live operational state about an account" — that is team intelligence and is a separate architectural question tracked in [ADR-0121](0121-team-intelligence-architecture.md) (Open). The publish framework remains the right channel for the reporting case; team intelligence is unsolved and explicitly acknowledged as such in [ADR-0116](0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md) "Update — 2026-04-20" section.
.docs/decisions/0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md:249:**Implications for this ADR:**
.docs/decisions/0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md:251:- The `PublishDestination` enum (§5) needs extensibility. S3, SharePoint, Confluence, and generic Webhook variants are plausible v1.4.2–v1.5.0 additions alongside P2. None of them should require an [ADR-0117](0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md) amendment — the protocol is stable; destinations are extensions.
.docs/decisions/0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md:262:Adversarial review + reference pass found the ADR's foundational claim — that `publish_to_p2` already exists — is false. The ADR is therefore not wrapping an existing capability; it is inventing the entire subsystem. Retarget accordingly.
.docs/decisions/0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md:268:**Revised target:** this ADR ships in **v1.4.2**, alongside the first real Publish ability. v1.4.0 ships only the **forward-looking interface definitions** (types, trait shapes, and boundary principles), and explicitly ships **no publish runtime**. This matches [ADR-0112](0112-migration-strategy-parallel-run-and-cutover.md)'s sequencing (Publish migrates last) and removes the inconsistency codex flagged.
.docs/decisions/0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md:273:- **v1.4.1:** outbox tables, worker pool (shares infrastructure with [ADR-0115](0115-signal-granularity-audit.md) invalidation workers per R1.3), idempotency enforcement.
.docs/decisions/0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md:278:Codex flagged: `commit_publish` writes an outbox entry and returns synchronously, but the original ADR implies the returned `PublishedRecord` includes destination refs (post ID). Those don't exist until delivery, which is async.
.docs/decisions/0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md:301:### R1.3 Reuse of [ADR-0115](0115-signal-granularity-audit.md) worker pool — conditional
.docs/decisions/0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md:303:Original §10 assumed the worker pool from [ADR-0115](0115-signal-granularity-audit.md) already existed. After R1 of that ADR it is acknowledged as new infrastructure with Phase 0 prerequisites. This ADR cannot reuse what hasn't landed.
.docs/decisions/0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md:305:**Revised:** worker pool dependency defers to v1.4.1. If ADR-0115 Phase 2 (durable invalidation jobs) has shipped by v1.4.1, this ADR's publish outbox workers are a second consumer of the same infrastructure. If not, a publish-specific worker pool ships alongside the outbox tables with an explicit "share with [ADR-0115](0115-signal-granularity-audit.md) when feasible" note.
.docs/decisions/0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md:346:Codex flagged an inter-ADR conflict with [ADR-0111](0111-surface-independent-ability-invocation.md), which states the MCP bridge refuses confirmation fields. The `AgentWithAuthority` shape in this ADR creates a policy-bypass surface.
.docs/decisions/0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md:348:**Revised:** in v1.4.0 and v1.4.1, every surface that invokes `commit_publish` **rejects** `ConfirmationToken` with `policy_source: AgentWithAuthority`. The shape exists in Rust for forward-compatibility; the validation gate in `commit_publish` returns `PublishError::AgentAuthorityNotYetSupported` if present. Tests cover both that the shape compiles and that invocation is rejected. First real implementation ships with the full authority-grant surface in v1.5.0 or later with a dedicated follow-on ADR.
.docs/decisions/0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md:365:Workers invoke this from an async runtime. The worker pool (shared with [ADR-0115](0115-signal-granularity-audit.md) or publish-specific) is async.
.docs/decisions/0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md:388:- `publish_to_p2` does not exist. ADR is retargeted to v1.4.2 for its implementation; v1.4.0–v1.4.1 lay foundations.
.docs/decisions/0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md:389:- `export_data_zip` at `src-tauri/src/export.rs` is the only existing external-write path and should be reviewed for compliance with this ADR's `PublishDestination::Export` variant as a first consumer — that verification is a v1.4.1 task.
.docs/decisions/0115-signal-granularity-audit.md:1:# ADR-0115: Signal Granularity, Policy Registry, and Durable Invalidation
.docs/decisions/0115-signal-granularity-audit.md:6:**Extends:** [ADR-0080](0080-signal-intelligence-architecture.md)
.docs/decisions/0115-signal-granularity-audit.md:7:**Related:** [ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md), [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md), [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md), [ADR-0110](0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md), [ADR-0113](0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md), [ADR-0114](0114-scoring-unification.md)
.docs/decisions/0115-signal-granularity-audit.md:12:[ADR-0080](0080-signal-intelligence-architecture.md) established the signal bus as the backbone of DailyOS intelligence: events fire, listeners react, Bayesian weights update, briefings recompute. It was designed when signals operated at entity granularity — "this account changed," "this person's title moved." Three in-flight changes break that granularity model:
.docs/decisions/0115-signal-granularity-audit.md:14:1. **Claim-level events.** [ADR-0113](0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md) introduces per-claim identity and a claim state machine. Every claim insert, supersede, retract, and trust recompute is an event someone wants to react to. That's claim granularity, orders of magnitude finer than entity granularity.
.docs/decisions/0115-signal-granularity-audit.md:15:2. **Ability-output events.** [ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) makes every capability a named, versioned ability producing an `AbilityOutput<T>`. Each output is cache-keyed and invalidation-tracked. "This output changed" is a new event class operating at ability-output granularity.
.docs/decisions/0115-signal-granularity-audit.md:16:3. **Trust recomputes as a high-volume source.** [ADR-0114](0114-scoring-unification.md) + [DOS-5](https://linear.app/a8c/issue/DOS-5) mean every claim gets a trust score that may shift on any of six factor inputs. Backfills and shadow-mode runs can produce millions of `ClaimTrustChanged` events in hours.
.docs/decisions/0115-signal-granularity-audit.md:20:- **Policy distributed across call sites.** Today the decision "emit this signal synchronously" vs "fire-and-forget" lives at each call site. Three overlapping functions (`emit`, `emit_signal`, `emit_signal_and_propagate`) coexist with inconsistent semantics. A maintainer who doesn't know the signal's invariants can silently pick the wrong variant, and the signal bus has no compile-time way to catch this.
.docs/decisions/0115-signal-granularity-audit.md:24:This ADR unifies granularity, moves propagation policy from call sites into a compile-time-checked registry, replaces depth-limited drops with durable invalidation jobs, and adds the back-pressure mechanisms needed for claim-level and ability-level events to be safe in production.
.docs/decisions/0115-signal-granularity-audit.md:92:Policy changes require editing the registry, which appears in diffs. Distributed call-site policy decisions are no longer possible.
.docs/decisions/0115-signal-granularity-audit.md:179:Under `ExecutionMode::Evaluate` ([ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md)):
.docs/decisions/0115-signal-granularity-audit.md:209:- **`emit_signal_with_override` is a documented escape hatch.** It must not become a normal path. PR review convention: any new use of `_with_override` requires an ADR amendment or a linked issue explaining the exception.
.docs/decisions/0115-signal-granularity-audit.md:217:- [ADR-0080](0080-signal-intelligence-architecture.md) is not replaced; it is the substrate this ADR refines. The Bayesian weight-update semantics remain as specified there.
.docs/decisions/0115-signal-granularity-audit.md:224:Adversarial review + reference pass found the ADR built on typed machinery that does not exist. Today's signal bus is stringly-typed, has four emission functions (not three), and lacks the enum/registry infrastructure this ADR assumes. Revision below.
.docs/decisions/0115-signal-granularity-audit.md:226:### R1.1 `SignalType` enum is a prerequisite, not a feature of this ADR
.docs/decisions/0115-signal-granularity-audit.md:232:- **Phase 0 (prerequisite work, lands before any policy registry):** Introduce `pub enum SignalType` in `src-tauri/src/signals/types.rs`. Initial variants mirror the existing string catalog found in signal-emitting code (`stakeholder_change`, `title_change`, `champion_risk`, `renewal_risk_escalation`, `engagement_warning`, `intelligence_confirmed`, `meeting_frequency_drop`, etc.). Plus the new claim-layer variants from [ADR-0113](0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md) (`ClaimAsserted`, `ClaimSuperseded`, `ClaimRetracted`, `ClaimTrustChanged`, `ClaimContradiction`). The enum is `#[non_exhaustive]` for forward compatibility.
.docs/decisions/0115-signal-granularity-audit.md:309:### R1.7 Evaluate-mode mutation conflict with ADR-0103/0104
.docs/decisions/0115-signal-granularity-audit.md:311:Codex flagged: [ADR-0103](0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md) and [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md) say non-Live modes must not mutate. The original §8 says `Evaluate` mode runs inline invalidation jobs — which can mutate caches or touch queues.
.docs/decisions/0115-signal-granularity-audit.md:313:**Revised:** under `Evaluate`, `emit_signal` writes to the in-memory ring buffer (no DB, no queue) and **does not run invalidation jobs**. Consumers that need to assert on invalidation behavior in tests use a separate `run_pending_invalidations_in_memory()` helper that a fixture explicitly invokes. This is analogous to how mutation-aware testing uses explicit flushes rather than implicit cascade. Matches [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md)'s no-side-effect contract.
.docs/decisions/0115-signal-granularity-audit.md:317:Codex flagged: the ADR consumes an `OutputId` concept that doesn't exist in code yet ([ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md)/[ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) are also doc-only).
.docs/decisions/0115-signal-granularity-audit.md:319:**Revised:** `OutputId` is defined in this ADR as a dependency placeholder and implemented in the abilities runtime work ([ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) implementation). Shape:
.docs/decisions/0115-signal-granularity-audit.md:329:This ADR's Phase 0 is blocked on the `AbilityOutput` + `Provenance` types landing from [ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) / [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) implementation. Document that dependency explicitly in [DOS-235](https://linear.app/a8c/issue/DOS-235)+.
