.docs/decisions/0090-user-entity-page-architecture.md:1:# ADR-0090: User Entity Page Architecture — Professional Context Surface, Context Entries, Two-Layer Priorities
.docs/decisions/0090-user-entity-page-architecture.md:6:**Extends:** ADR-0089 (User Entity and Professional Context Layer)
.docs/decisions/0090-user-entity-page-architecture.md:7:**Complements:** ADR-0079 (Role Presets) — role presets govern vocabulary; this ADR governs page structure, input model, and role-shaped section prominence.
.docs/decisions/0090-user-entity-page-architecture.md:13:The user entity established in ADR-0089 needs a dedicated page surface — not inside Settings, but its own first-class area with the same editorial treatment as account and person detail pages. ADR-0089 defined what the user entity contains (professional context, declared priorities, attached documents). This ADR defines how that content is organised on a page, what interaction patterns it uses, how role presets shape what's prominent, and what new input types are introduced.
.docs/decisions/0090-user-entity-page-architecture.md:29:### What ADR-0089 left underspecified
.docs/decisions/0090-user-entity-page-architecture.md:31:ADR-0089 established the data model and the intelligence integration. It did not specify:
.docs/decisions/0090-user-entity-page-architecture.md:38:This ADR fills those gaps.
.docs/decisions/0090-user-entity-page-architecture.md:145:v0.14.1 is exclusively expansion work: writing role-specific vocabulary, placeholder text, and named playbook labels for all nine presets defined in ADR-0079. This is deliberate sequencing — get the architecture and interaction model right on one role before expanding. The expansion is vocabulary authoring work, not architecture work.
.docs/decisions/0090-user-entity-page-architecture.md:163:**The success plan report (v0.15.0) depends on this ADR being fully implemented.** The user entity page is where the user populates the second side of the success plan intersection (what I deliver + what the account wants = success plan). Without a well-populated user entity, success plans are structurally correct but professionally hollow.
.docs/decisions/0082-entity-generic-prep-pipeline.md:1:# ADR-0082: Entity-Generic Prep Pipeline
.docs/decisions/0082-entity-generic-prep-pipeline.md:7:**Builds on:** [ADR-0057](0057-entity-intelligence-architecture.md) (entity intelligence), [ADR-0080](0080-signal-intelligence-architecture.md) (signal intelligence), [ADR-0081](0081-event-driven-meeting-intelligence.md) (event-driven meeting intelligence)
.docs/decisions/0082-entity-generic-prep-pipeline.md:41:### What ADR-0081 Requires
.docs/decisions/0082-entity-generic-prep-pipeline.md:43:ADR-0081 (Event-Driven Meeting Intelligence, 0.13.0) declares: "Every meeting gets intelligence." It proposes entity intelligence, person intelligence, and minimal intelligence tiers. It assumes the prep pipeline can resolve to any entity type and inject the right context.
.docs/decisions/0082-entity-generic-prep-pipeline.md:63:This is the three-file pattern from ADR-0057 applied to people -- but only for people you have 1:1 relationships with, not for every contact in the system.
.docs/decisions/0082-entity-generic-prep-pipeline.md:175:People get the three-file pattern (ADR-0057) when they have a 1:1 relationship with the user. This is triggered by meeting classification: when a meeting is detected as a 1:1 (2 attendees, recurring, one is the user), the other person becomes a relationship entity.
.docs/decisions/0082-entity-generic-prep-pipeline.md:228:Entity resolution signals (ADR-0080) priority:
.docs/decisions/0082-entity-generic-prep-pipeline.md:241:User corrections (priority 1) always win. The rest fuse via Bayesian combination (ADR-0080 Section 4).
.docs/decisions/0082-entity-generic-prep-pipeline.md:279:- **ADR-0081 unblocked.** The 0.13.0 meeting intelligence sprint can focus on intelligence lifecycle, not pipeline plumbing.
.docs/decisions/0082-entity-generic-prep-pipeline.md:319:- ADR-0057: Entity intelligence architecture (three-file pattern, entity-generic intelligence)
.docs/decisions/0082-entity-generic-prep-pipeline.md:320:- ADR-0080: Signal intelligence architecture (signal fusion, entity resolution cascade)
.docs/decisions/0082-entity-generic-prep-pipeline.md:321:- ADR-0081: Event-driven meeting intelligence (intelligence lifecycle, every meeting gets intelligence)
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:1:# ADR-0103: Maintenance Ability Safety Constraints
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:6:**Extends:** [ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md), [ADR-0101](0101-service-boundary-enforcement.md)  
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:7:**Depends on:** [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md) (prerequisite for mode enforcement in §3, audit in §8, transactions in §2)  
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:8:**Related:** [ADR-0080](0080-signal-intelligence-architecture.md), [ADR-0098](0098-data-governance-source-aware-lifecycle.md)
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:12:[ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) introduces four ability categories: Read, Transform, Publish, and Maintenance. Three of the four have naturally bounded risk surfaces:
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:20:- Mutate internal state — the entity graph, signal weights, computed trajectories, derived indices.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:25:Service-layer mutations ([ADR-0101](0101-service-boundary-enforcement.md)) are bounded by user action: each mutation corresponds to something a user or agent explicitly did, the blast radius is contained to that action, and errors surface immediately to the caller. Maintenance mutations have none of those natural constraints. A bug in a maintenance ability is the single largest new risk surface introduced by [ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md).
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:37:This ADR specifies those constraints.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:41:Every ability in the Maintenance category operates under all rules of [ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) plus the eleven constraints below, covering idempotency, transactional boundaries, execution-mode and dry-run semantics, multi-dimensional change budgets, signal containment, actor policy, blast radius with user-confirmed global, operational audit records, snapshot-diff evaluation, scoped observability, and compile-time separation. These constraints are enforced by type system, registry metadata, runtime checks, and review discipline — not by convention.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:58:ADR-0101's `with_transaction` is synchronous; maintenance abilities need an async variant because they invoke async service functions (network I/O, intelligence provider calls inside the transaction are forbidden, but async DB functions are common). [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md) introduces `with_transaction_async` built on SQLite's write-serialization. Maintenance uses it:
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:101:This ADR relies on [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md) which makes `ExecutionMode` load-bearing in the service layer. The three modes (`Live`, `Simulate`, `Evaluate`) have precise and non-overlapping semantics, summarized below. Maintenance abilities additionally honor a `dry_run` input flag that is orthogonal to mode. All four operational paths rely on a common **plan/apply split**: abilities compute a planned mutation set via non-mutating planning calls, then either apply the plan (Live, live-apply phase) or short-circuit before application (Simulate, Evaluate, or Live+dry_run).
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:115:- **`Simulate` vs `Evaluate`.** Both reject mutations and signals. `Evaluate` additionally supplies a deterministic clock, seeded RNG, and replay providers (per [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md) §6) so snapshot-diff scoring is stable across runs. `Simulate` is a developer-facing "let me try this without side effects" mode where determinism is not guaranteed and tracer output is verbose but not normalized.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:120:- Service-layer mode enforcement is specified in [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md).
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:139:    // AbilityPolicy fields (canonical schema per ADR-0102 §7.1):
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:144:    // Maintenance-specific metadata (this ADR):
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:154:    // Composition metadata (per ADR-0102 §7.1):
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:169:| `account_scope` | 200 | 2,000 | 500 | 10 | ADR amendment |
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:170:| `global` | — | — | — | — | ADR amendment; no defaults |
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:176:Signals emitted during maintenance operations preserve their original `data_source` per [ADR-0098](0098-data-governance-source-aware-lifecycle.md) (e.g., `glean`, `salesforce`, `user`, `ai`) — **never** overwritten with a synthetic "Maintenance" source, which would violate the data-origin model and break purge-on-revocation (Codex finding #15). Instead, signals emitted from inside a maintenance invocation carry two additional fields that describe the operational context without conflating it with data provenance:
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:180:    // ... existing ADR-0080/0098 fields unchanged ...
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:181:    pub data_source: DataSource,                       // original source, per ADR-0098
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:183:    // New fields added in this ADR:
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:191:- `PropagationEngine` ([ADR-0080](0080-signal-intelligence-architecture.md)) treats signals with `maintenance_invocation_id.is_some()` as containment-limited by default: their `propagation_depth_limit` is set to 1 unless the ability's registry metadata declares otherwise.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:208:// Canonical AbilityPolicy schema per ADR-0102 §7.1
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:217:Maintenance abilities default to `allowed_actors: [System]`. Agents and users invoking a maintenance ability through the registry receive `Error::ActorNotAllowed` at the boundary, before the ability executes. Opt-in to `Agent` or `User` invocation requires explicit ADR amendment and a documented justification.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:219:**Why this matters:** An MCP agent enumerates the registry as its tool catalog. Without actor policy, every maintenance operation becomes an agent-invokable tool. Default-deny for agents on maintenance is the safe posture. Registry introspection is actor-filtered per [ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) §7.4 — agents do not see maintenance ability names or schemas at all.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:221:Actor policy alone is insufficient in-process: a direct Rust import of a maintenance ability function would still execute regardless of the registry policy. Compile-time separation (§11) closes that gap post-v1.4.0. Until then, review discipline and the typed registry invocation paths from [ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) §7.2 prevent accidental bypass.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:265:    // Provenance envelope — shape defined by ADR-0105
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:266:    pub provenance: ProvenanceOrMasked,          // Full provenance or masked marker if sources revoked
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:283:// Masking rules and shape defined by ADR-0108.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:284:pub enum ProvenanceOrMasked {
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:285:    Full(Provenance),
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:286:    Masked(ProvenanceMasked),
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:292:**Two records per invocation, always.** Both records are produced by the registry's maintenance-ability wrapper (not by the ability body) after the plan and apply phases specified in [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md) §3.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:326:- **Fixture:** an input state snapshot (entity states, signals, weights) captured from anonymized local data, with fixture governance per [ADR-0107](0107-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md) (source tagging, retention, purge-on-revocation).
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:331:This is more expensive to set up than transform-ability eval but becomes tractable once §3 (ExecutionMode enforcement and dry-run contract) is in place. [ADR-0107](0107-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md) specifies the harness mechanics and fixture governance.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:337:Every maintenance invocation produces structured telemetry captured by the `AbilityTracer` from [ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) §5, with maintenance-specific fields persisted to the `maintenance_audit` table (see §8):
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:355:4. **Eval harness regression gate** — CI catches quality regressions (via [ADR-0107](0107-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md)). This is the only "alerting" — and it fires pre-merge, not in production.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:392:5. **Signal bus integrity preserved.** Maintenance-emitted signals keep their true `data_source` ([ADR-0098](0098-data-governance-source-aware-lifecycle.md)), with containment tracked via orthogonal metadata. Purge cascades and source reliability weighting continue to work.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:400:2. **Service layer takes on mode-checking responsibility.** Every mutation function honors `ExecutionMode` (per [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md)). Touches a lot of code but is mechanical.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:404:6. **Eval-harness complexity increases.** Snapshot-diff evaluation is mechanically more involved than output scoring. [ADR-0107](0107-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md) must account for this.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:409:2. **Mis-categorized abilities.** A transform-shaped operation gets filed as maintenance (or vice versa) to bypass or escape category-specific discipline. Mitigation: category determined by call-graph effect per [ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) §3 — static check at registration.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:411:4. **Actor policy drift.** Over time, pressure to let agents invoke maintenance ("but I promise it's safe") erodes the default-deny posture. Mitigation: actor policy changes require ADR amendment, not a one-line registry edit.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:415:8. **Loophole for "simple" maintenance.** The ADR allows trivial cross-service cleanup operations to live in a scheduled job rather than as an ability. This could be abused to file anything as "simple" and skip the constraints (Codex finding #38). Mitigation: "simple" is defined narrowly — single-service mutation, no synthesis, no external calls. Anything touching multiple services or emitting signals is an ability. Review enforces.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:416:9. **Mode contract violations.** A maintenance ability or a composed sub-ability accidentally writes in Simulate/Evaluate mode. Mitigation: enforcement lives in the service layer ([ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md)) — abilities cannot bypass it.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:421:- [ADR-0102: Abilities as the Runtime Contract](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) — Establishes the ability pattern this ADR constrains. Maintenance is one of four categories; this ADR defines its safety envelope.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:422:- [ADR-0101: Service Boundary Enforcement](0101-service-boundary-enforcement.md) — Services remain the mutation mechanism. Maintenance abilities orchestrate services; they do not bypass them. Rule 3 (multi-write transactionality) extends to the cross-service case here via `with_transaction_async`.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:423:- [ADR-0080: Signal Intelligence Architecture](0080-signal-intelligence-architecture.md) — Signal schema and propagation engine. This ADR adds `maintenance_invocation_id` and `propagation_depth_limit` fields for containment.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:424:- [ADR-0098: Data Governance — Source-Aware Lifecycle](0098-data-governance-source-aware-lifecycle.md) — `data_source` remains the canonical origin marker. Maintenance context does not overwrite it; orthogonal fields preserve lifecycle semantics.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:425:- **[ADR-0104: ExecutionMode and Mode-Aware Services](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md)** — Prerequisite. Specifies how `ExecutionMode` flows through `ServiceContext`, how mutation functions honor it, and `with_transaction_async` primitive.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:426:- **ADR-0105 (forthcoming): Provenance as First-Class Output** — Defines the provenance shape that maintenance abilities populate (separately from the operational audit records in §8 of this ADR).
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:427:- **ADR-0107 (forthcoming): Evaluation Harness for Abilities** — Specifies the snapshot-diff evaluation mechanism referenced in §9, including fixture governance and anonymization rules.
.docs/decisions/0089-user-entity-and-professional-context.md:1:# ADR-0089: User Entity and Professional Context Layer
.docs/decisions/0089-user-entity-and-professional-context.md:6:**Complements:** ADR-0079 (Role Presets) — role presets govern vocabulary and field structure; this ADR governs professional context and intelligence framing. They are not alternatives.
.docs/decisions/0089-user-entity-and-professional-context.md:7:**Complements:** ADR-0086 (Intelligence as Shared Service) — user context becomes a shared input to every entity enrichment prompt, alongside entity signals and meeting history.
.docs/decisions/0089-user-entity-and-professional-context.md:23:**The role preset (ADR-0079) does not solve this.** Role presets shape vocabulary — "churn risk" vs "deal stalled" vs "scope creep." They do not capture what makes the user's product or service valuable, what their customers are trying to achieve, or what the user themselves is trying to accomplish. A CSM using the Customer Success preset and a CSM at a different company using the same preset have entirely different professional contexts that the vocabulary cannot express.
.docs/decisions/0089-user-entity-and-professional-context.md:102:### 6. Relationship to role preset (ADR-0079)
.docs/decisions/0089-user-entity-and-professional-context.md:106:| Dimension | Role Preset (ADR-0079) | User Entity (this ADR) |
.docs/decisions/0089-user-entity-and-professional-context.md:128:- The success plan report type (v0.15.0) is only possible because of this ADR. Without the user entity, a success plan produced by DailyOS is structurally correct but professionally hollow.
.docs/decisions/0028-structured-document-schemas.md:1:# ADR-0028: Structured document schemas (JSON-first templates)
.docs/decisions/0028-structured-document-schemas.md:8:Account dashboards, success plans, and other structured documents need mechanical updates (e.g., "Last Contact" date, "Recent Wins" list). Updating these via regex on markdown is fragile (per ADR-0004 rationale).
.docs/decisions/0028-structured-document-schemas.md:12:Structured documents get JSON schemas. Rust reads JSON, applies structured updates, writes JSON back. Markdown is optionally regenerated from JSON for human readability. Extends ADR-0004 pattern from `_today/` ephemeral data to all persistent structured documents.
.docs/decisions/0002-frontend-first-implementation.md:1:# ADR-0002: Frontend-first implementation
.docs/decisions/0112-migration-strategy-parallel-run-and-cutover.md:1:# ADR-0112: Migration Strategy — Parallel Run and Cutover
.docs/decisions/0112-migration-strategy-parallel-run-and-cutover.md:6:**Extends:** [ADR-0101](0101-service-boundary-enforcement.md), [ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md), [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md)  
.docs/decisions/0112-migration-strategy-parallel-run-and-cutover.md:7:**Depends on:** [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md), [ADR-0107](0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md), [ADR-0110](0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md), [ADR-0111](0111-surface-independent-ability-invocation.md)
.docs/decisions/0112-migration-strategy-parallel-run-and-cutover.md:11:[ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) §14 describes per-capability migration but does not specify parallel-run validation, cutover criteria, rollback, or the three specific substrate migrations that ship as part of v1.4.0:
.docs/decisions/0112-migration-strategy-parallel-run-and-cutover.md:13:1. `ServiceContext` amendment per [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md) — mode-aware services, `check_mutation_allowed()` added to ~60 mutation functions, clock/RNG injection.
.docs/decisions/0112-migration-strategy-parallel-run-and-cutover.md:14:2. `source` → `data_source` schema rename per [ADR-0107](0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md).
.docs/decisions/0112-migration-strategy-parallel-run-and-cutover.md:15:3. Synthesis-marker backfill per [ADR-0107](0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md) §6 — tagging existing stored AI output so [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md)'s trust model works retroactively.
.docs/decisions/0112-migration-strategy-parallel-run-and-cutover.md:17:This ADR specifies the per-capability migration flow, the three substrate migrations, parallel-run validation, cutover criteria, and rollback procedures.
.docs/decisions/0112-migration-strategy-parallel-run-and-cutover.md:27:**Stage 1 — Ability implementation.** A new ability module is added under `src-tauri/src/abilities/` per [ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) §2. Includes planner function, apply function (for Maintenance), provenance builder usage per [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md), and declared metadata (`mutates`, `composes`, policy).
.docs/decisions/0112-migration-strategy-parallel-run-and-cutover.md:29:**Stage 2 — Eval fixture.** At least one evaluation fixture per ability with expected output per [ADR-0110](0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md). CI runs the fixture on every PR touching the ability.
.docs/decisions/0112-migration-strategy-parallel-run-and-cutover.md:45:- Tauri command becomes a thin wrapper around `invoke_ability(name, input)` per [ADR-0111](0111-surface-independent-ability-invocation.md).
.docs/decisions/0112-migration-strategy-parallel-run-and-cutover.md:46:- MCP tool is removed from hand-written registration; the registry-derived tool takes over.
.docs/decisions/0112-migration-strategy-parallel-run-and-cutover.md:53:#### 2.1 `ServiceContext` and Mode-Awareness ([ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md))
.docs/decisions/0112-migration-strategy-parallel-run-and-cutover.md:61:- `ExternalClients` wrapper infrastructure added per [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md) §2.
.docs/decisions/0112-migration-strategy-parallel-run-and-cutover.md:65:- Every mutation function in `services/` adds `ctx.check_mutation_allowed()?` as its first line (~60 call sites per [ADR-0101](0101-service-boundary-enforcement.md) audit).
.docs/decisions/0112-migration-strategy-parallel-run-and-cutover.md:72:- `ServiceContext::new_evaluate` wired into [ADR-0110](0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md) fixture harness.
.docs/decisions/0112-migration-strategy-parallel-run-and-cutover.md:77:#### 2.2 `source` → `data_source` Schema Rename ([ADR-0107](0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md))
.docs/decisions/0112-migration-strategy-parallel-run-and-cutover.md:97:#### 2.3 Synthesis-Marker Backfill ([ADR-0107](0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md) §6)
.docs/decisions/0112-migration-strategy-parallel-run-and-cutover.md:107:- Read paths consult `synthesized_fields` when building `SourceAttribution.synthesis_marker` per [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) §4.
.docs/decisions/0112-migration-strategy-parallel-run-and-cutover.md:120:5. `detect_risk_shift` — validates Transform + trajectory consumption from [ADR-0109](0109-temporal-primitives-in-the-entity-graph.md).
.docs/decisions/0112-migration-strategy-parallel-run-and-cutover.md:154:- All ADRs 0102–0112 accepted.
.docs/decisions/0112-migration-strategy-parallel-run-and-cutover.md:191:1. **Parallel-run masking.** A persistent divergence is explained away as "acceptable" rather than addressed. Mitigation: divergence reviews require a documented decision (accept, fix, rebaseline); no silent dismissals.
.docs/decisions/0112-migration-strategy-parallel-run-and-cutover.md:193:3. **Release cadence slip.** v1.4.2 cutover slips because full migration drags on. Mitigation: the ADR framework tolerates hybrid state; hand-written and registry-derived paths coexist indefinitely if needed, at the cost of duplicated maintenance.
.docs/decisions/0112-migration-strategy-parallel-run-and-cutover.md:199:- [ADR-0101: Service Boundary Enforcement](0101-service-boundary-enforcement.md) — §2.1 migration completes ADR-0101's Phase 3 goals.
.docs/decisions/0112-migration-strategy-parallel-run-and-cutover.md:200:- [ADR-0102: Abilities as the Runtime Contract](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) — §14 migration-from-current-architecture refined here.
.docs/decisions/0112-migration-strategy-parallel-run-and-cutover.md:201:- [ADR-0103: Maintenance Ability Safety Constraints](0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md) — Maintenance capability migration order in §3.
.docs/decisions/0112-migration-strategy-parallel-run-and-cutover.md:202:- [ADR-0104: ExecutionMode and Mode-Aware Services](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md) — §2.1 substrate migration.
.docs/decisions/0112-migration-strategy-parallel-run-and-cutover.md:203:- [ADR-0105: Provenance as First-Class Output](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) — §2.3 synthesis-marker backfill enables `contains_stored_synthesis`.
.docs/decisions/0112-migration-strategy-parallel-run-and-cutover.md:204:- [ADR-0107: Source Taxonomy Alignment](0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md) — §2.2 `source` → `data_source` rename; §2.3 synthesis markers.
