.docs/decisions/0098-data-governance-source-aware-lifecycle.md:70:- AI-generated intelligence — this is DailyOS's analysis, not raw source data. However, intelligence that was primarily derived from purged source data should be flagged for re-enrichment
.docs/decisions/0098-data-governance-source-aware-lifecycle.md:72:- Actions and decisions — the user's work product
.docs/decisions/0098-data-governance-source-aware-lifecycle.md:82:ADR-0094's audit log already tracks data access events. Extend it to cover:
.docs/decisions/0098-data-governance-source-aware-lifecycle.md:154:3. **I487 (Glean signal emission)** emits signals with `source = "glean"`. Purge-on-revocation deletes Glean signals but doesn't cascade to intelligence derived from those signals (intelligence is flagged for re-enrichment instead).
.docs/decisions/0098-data-governance-source-aware-lifecycle.md:179:This ADR must be implemented (at minimum: source tagging on entity_people, purge_source function, Settings UI for disconnect-with-purge) before:
.docs/decisions/0100-glean-first-intelligence-architecture.md:1:# ADR-0100 — Glean-First Intelligence Architecture
.docs/decisions/0100-glean-first-intelligence-architecture.md:5:**Supersedes:** Portions of ADR-0095 (dual-mode context — updates the "dual" to "Glean-primary")
.docs/decisions/0100-glean-first-intelligence-architecture.md:12:When Glean is connected, it becomes the **primary intelligence computation engine**. Claude Code PTY calls become the **fallback for users without Glean**. This inverts the original dual-mode design (ADR-0095) where local was primary and Glean was supplementary.
.docs/decisions/0100-glean-first-intelligence-architecture.md:22:ADR-0095 designed Glean as an additive context source — search results appended to local context, with the PTY call doing all synthesis. The I559 validation spike (2026-03-14) discovered:
.docs/decisions/0100-glean-first-intelligence-architecture.md:33:| Capability | Before (ADR-0095) | After (ADR-0100) |
.docs/decisions/0100-glean-first-intelligence-architecture.md:181:6. **ADR-0098 data governance applies.** Glean-sourced data is tagged with `data_source = "glean"`. `purge_source(DataSource::Glean)` removes all Glean-sourced data on disconnect.
.docs/decisions/0100-glean-first-intelligence-architecture.md:208:## Related ADRs
.docs/decisions/0100-glean-first-intelligence-architecture.md:210:- **ADR-0095** (Dual-mode context) — this ADR updates the "dual" from "local primary + Glean supplementary" to "Glean primary + local fallback"
.docs/decisions/0100-glean-first-intelligence-architecture.md:211:- **ADR-0097** (Health scoring) — unchanged architecturally, but gains richer inputs from Glean-sourced signals
.docs/decisions/0100-glean-first-intelligence-architecture.md:212:- **ADR-0098** (Data governance) — Glean data tagged and purgeable per existing design
.docs/decisions/0100-glean-first-intelligence-architecture.md:213:- **ADR-0099** (Remote-first) — WITHDRAWN. ADR-0100 achieves the benefits of remote intelligence without the server-sync architecture that ADR-0099 proposed.
.docs/decisions/0039-feature-toggle-architecture.md:1:# ADR-0039: Feature toggle architecture
.docs/decisions/0039-feature-toggle-architecture.md:5:**Evolves:** [ADR-0026](0026-extension-architecture.md) (extension architecture)
.docs/decisions/0039-feature-toggle-architecture.md:9:ADR-0026 established extensions as profile-activated module bundles. This works for distribution boundaries but doesn't give users granular control. A CS user might want account tracking but not post-meeting capture. The monolithic extension model doesn't support this.
