.docs/decisions/0109-temporal-primitives-in-the-entity-graph.md:1:# ADR-0109: Temporal Primitives in the Entity Graph
.docs/decisions/0109-temporal-primitives-in-the-entity-graph.md:6:**Extends:** [ADR-0082](0082-entity-generic-prep-pipeline.md), [ADR-0097](0097-account-health-scoring-architecture.md), [ADR-0088](0088-people-relationship-network-intelligence.md)  
.docs/decisions/0109-temporal-primitives-in-the-entity-graph.md:7:**Consumed by:** [ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) (Read abilities), [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) (provenance references trajectory snapshots)
.docs/decisions/0109-temporal-primitives-in-the-entity-graph.md:30:    HealthCurve,              // Time series of health score dimensions per entity (ADR-0097)
.docs/decisions/0109-temporal-primitives-in-the-entity-graph.md:34:    RelationshipStrength,      // Per-pair-of-people strength over time (ADR-0088)
.docs/decisions/0109-temporal-primitives-in-the-entity-graph.md:35:    Custom(&'static str),      // Registered extensions require ADR amendment
.docs/decisions/0109-temporal-primitives-in-the-entity-graph.md:49:    pub source_refs: Vec<SourceRef>,      // Points into provenance sources per ADR-0105
.docs/decisions/0109-temporal-primitives-in-the-entity-graph.md:55:- `HealthCurve` → `T = HealthScoreSnapshot` (six dimensions per [ADR-0097](0097-account-health-scoring-architecture.md), point-in-time)
.docs/decisions/0109-temporal-primitives-in-the-entity-graph.md:85:These are Maintenance abilities subject to [ADR-0103](0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md): they have budgets, idempotency tests, dry-run support, and audit records.
.docs/decisions/0109-temporal-primitives-in-the-entity-graph.md:118:### 5. Provenance Integration
.docs/decisions/0109-temporal-primitives-in-the-entity-graph.md:120:Each `DataPoint<T>` carries `source_refs` pointing to the signals and entities that contributed to it. When an ability composes a trajectory and uses it in synthesis, its provenance includes the trajectory's source references transitively via `SourceRef::Child` per [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) §5.
.docs/decisions/0109-temporal-primitives-in-the-entity-graph.md:124:Trajectory tables are derived from signals and entities. When a source is revoked per [ADR-0107](0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md), the trajectory data points derived from that source are invalidated: the row is marked with `source_invalidated_at` and `get_entity_context` filters it out. A subsequent refresh maintenance run recomputes the trajectory with remaining sources.
.docs/decisions/0109-temporal-primitives-in-the-entity-graph.md:135:- `HealthCurve` — depends on [ADR-0097](0097-account-health-scoring-architecture.md) being accepted
.docs/decisions/0109-temporal-primitives-in-the-entity-graph.md:139:- `RelationshipStrength` — requires pair-level modeling from [ADR-0088](0088-people-relationship-network-intelligence.md)
.docs/decisions/0109-temporal-primitives-in-the-entity-graph.md:151:6. **Invalidation honors lifecycle.** Source revocation cleanly invalidates derived trajectory points.
.docs/decisions/0109-temporal-primitives-in-the-entity-graph.md:163:3. **Schema evolution of `HealthScoreSnapshot`.** [ADR-0097](0097-account-health-scoring-architecture.md)'s dimensions may change. Mitigation: stored snapshots retain the dimensions as of their computation time; readers handle evolution via ADR-0097's own schema versioning.
.docs/decisions/0109-temporal-primitives-in-the-entity-graph.md:164:4. **Phase 3 `Custom` extensions fragment the taxonomy.** Mitigation: `Custom(...)` requires ADR amendment per §1; no casual additions.
.docs/decisions/0109-temporal-primitives-in-the-entity-graph.md:168:- [ADR-0082: Entity-Generic Prep Pipeline](0082-entity-generic-prep-pipeline.md) — Entity abstraction on which trajectories hang.
.docs/decisions/0109-temporal-primitives-in-the-entity-graph.md:169:- [ADR-0097: Account Health Scoring Architecture](0097-account-health-scoring-architecture.md) — `HealthScoreSnapshot` dimensions.
.docs/decisions/0109-temporal-primitives-in-the-entity-graph.md:170:- [ADR-0088: People Relationship Network Intelligence](0088-people-relationship-network-intelligence.md) — `RelationshipStrength` primitive.
.docs/decisions/0109-temporal-primitives-in-the-entity-graph.md:171:- [ADR-0102: Abilities as the Runtime Contract](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) — `get_entity_context` consumes trajectory bundle.
.docs/decisions/0109-temporal-primitives-in-the-entity-graph.md:172:- [ADR-0105: Provenance as First-Class Output](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) — `DataPoint.source_refs` integrate with provenance.
.docs/decisions/0109-temporal-primitives-in-the-entity-graph.md:173:- [ADR-0107: Source Taxonomy Alignment](0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md) — Source revocation invalidates trajectory data points.
.docs/decisions/0109-temporal-primitives-in-the-entity-graph.md:174:- [ADR-0103: Maintenance Ability Safety Constraints](0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md) — Refresh abilities operate under maintenance safety.
.docs/decisions/0119-runtime-evaluator-pass-for-transform-abilities.md:1:# ADR-0119: Runtime Evaluator Pass for Transform Abilities
.docs/decisions/0119-runtime-evaluator-pass-for-transform-abilities.md:6:**Extends:** [ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md), [ADR-0110](0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md)
.docs/decisions/0119-runtime-evaluator-pass-for-transform-abilities.md:7:**Related:** [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md), [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md), [ADR-0106](0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md), [ADR-0118](0118-dailyos-as-ai-harness-principles-and-residual-gaps.md) (Gap A)
.docs/decisions/0119-runtime-evaluator-pass-for-transform-abilities.md:8:**Resolves:** [ADR-0118](0118-dailyos-as-ai-harness-principles-and-residual-gaps.md) Gap A — Runtime evaluator pass
.docs/decisions/0119-runtime-evaluator-pass-for-transform-abilities.md:12:[ADR-0110](0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md) defines an LLM-as-judge rubric scoring model for Transform abilities. It runs **at test time** on fixtures to gate PR merges. This catches prompt regressions and logic bugs, but it cannot see production inputs and does not run on live invocations. When a Transform ability produces a bad output in production — an insight that reads confidently but is wrong, a brief that misses the meeting's actual context, a stakeholder call that mis-classifies intent — the output passes structural verification ([`validation.rs`](../../src-tauri/src/intelligence/validation.rs), [`consistency.rs`](../../src-tauri/src/intelligence/consistency.rs)) and reaches the user unchallenged.
.docs/decisions/0119-runtime-evaluator-pass-for-transform-abilities.md:14:[ADR-0118](0118-dailyos-as-ai-harness-principles-and-residual-gaps.md) Gap A names this explicitly: the generator/evaluator separation Anthropic identifies as the single strongest harness lever exists only at test time. Runtime has a generator and deterministic sanity checks, but no evaluator.
.docs/decisions/0119-runtime-evaluator-pass-for-transform-abilities.md:16:This ADR specifies the runtime evaluator pass: an additional LLM invocation after a Transform ability's primary output, scoring the output against a rubric, and triggering re-prompt on low scores. It is intentionally simple — reuses [ADR-0110](0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md)'s rubric definitions, reuses [ADR-0106](0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md)'s provider interface, and adds no new storage beyond a trace row per evaluation.
.docs/decisions/0119-runtime-evaluator-pass-for-transform-abilities.md:62:### 2. Rubric source — reuse [ADR-0110](0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md)'s `quality.toml`
.docs/decisions/0119-runtime-evaluator-pass-for-transform-abilities.md:64:Rubric dimensions, thresholds, and judge prompts are defined exactly once — in the `quality.toml` files under `src-tauri/tests/abilities/{ability_name}/evals/` per [ADR-0110](0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md) §5.
.docs/decisions/0119-runtime-evaluator-pass-for-transform-abilities.md:70:Per [ADR-0110](0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md) §5, judge prompts produce structured rubric scores. The runtime evaluator uses the same judge prompt:
.docs/decisions/0119-runtime-evaluator-pass-for-transform-abilities.md:95:`composite = weighted sum of dimension scores / max possible * 100`. Threshold per ability in `quality.toml`. Judge output is itself fingerprinted ([ADR-0106](0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md)) and stored for audit.
.docs/decisions/0119-runtime-evaluator-pass-for-transform-abilities.md:116:Judge model tier defaults to `fast` ([ADR-0091](0091-intelligence-provider-abstraction.md) tier enum); a smaller, cheaper model is sufficient for rubric scoring and keeps cost in check. Tier override per ability is supported for cases where the judge itself needs capability.
.docs/decisions/0119-runtime-evaluator-pass-for-transform-abilities.md:137:The retry's provenance envelope ([ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md)) records:
.docs/decisions/0119-runtime-evaluator-pass-for-transform-abilities.md:177:- Debugging — [ADR-0118](0118-dailyos-as-ai-harness-principles-and-residual-gaps.md) Gap E's debug trace surface consumes this.
.docs/decisions/0119-runtime-evaluator-pass-for-transform-abilities.md:183:Under `ExecutionMode::Evaluate` ([ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md)), the evaluator is **not** invoked. Fixture replay returns deterministic primary outputs; re-running the evaluator against them would produce scores that depend on the current state of the judge model and rubric, breaking replay determinism. Test-time scoring is [ADR-0110](0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md)'s job; runtime scoring is this ADR's job. They do not overlap.
.docs/decisions/0119-runtime-evaluator-pass-for-transform-abilities.md:187:Under `ExecutionMode::DryRun` ([ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md)), the evaluator runs but any retry is also dry-run — the retried output is a preview, not a write.
.docs/decisions/0119-runtime-evaluator-pass-for-transform-abilities.md:191:The evaluator composite score is distinct from trust score. Trust ([ADR-0114](0114-scoring-unification.md), [DOS-5](https://linear.app/a8c/issue/DOS-5)) answers "how much do we believe this claim's inputs." Evaluator composite answers "how good is the synthesis the ability produced." Both feed `TrustAssessment`:
.docs/decisions/0119-runtime-evaluator-pass-for-transform-abilities.md:194:- Evaluator composite is stored on the ability output (not per-claim) via a new `output_quality_score` field in the Provenance envelope ([ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) amendment required).
.docs/decisions/0119-runtime-evaluator-pass-for-transform-abilities.md:197:The amendment to [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) adds:
.docs/decisions/0119-runtime-evaluator-pass-for-transform-abilities.md:200:pub struct Provenance {
.docs/decisions/0119-runtime-evaluator-pass-for-transform-abilities.md:209:[ADR-0118](0118-dailyos-as-ai-harness-principles-and-residual-gaps.md) Gap A noted that runtime evaluator becomes more powerful with claim history. Now that [ADR-0113](0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md) specifies append-only claim history via supersede pointers, the evaluator can include a **stability-as-rubric-dimension** option:
.docs/decisions/0119-runtime-evaluator-pass-for-transform-abilities.md:224:- **v1.4.0:** ADR proposed; no implementation. Enrichment research spike ([DOS-241](https://linear.app/a8c/issue/DOS-241)) includes evaluator integration in its design doc.
.docs/decisions/0119-runtime-evaluator-pass-for-transform-abilities.md:227:- **v1.5.1+:** Stability dimension activates for abilities with sufficient claim history (requires [ADR-0113](0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md) R1.9 enrichment consolidation complete).
.docs/decisions/0119-runtime-evaluator-pass-for-transform-abilities.md:234:- **Closes [ADR-0118](0118-dailyos-as-ai-harness-principles-and-residual-gaps.md) Gap A.** Generator/evaluator separation operational at runtime, not just test time. Matches Anthropic's identified single-strongest harness lever.
.docs/decisions/0119-runtime-evaluator-pass-for-transform-abilities.md:235:- **Rubric reused from [ADR-0110](0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md).** One rubric definition; test-time and runtime cannot drift.
.docs/decisions/0119-runtime-evaluator-pass-for-transform-abilities.md:237:- **Stability-as-confidence available** once claim history lands. Composes cleanly with [ADR-0113](0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md).
.docs/decisions/0119-runtime-evaluator-pass-for-transform-abilities.md:239:- **Mode-aware.** Evaluator does not run in `Evaluate` mode; test-time scoring ([ADR-0110](0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md)) is unchanged.
.docs/decisions/0119-runtime-evaluator-pass-for-transform-abilities.md:244:- **Evaluator can have its own bad days.** A misbehaving judge model produces false lows (unnecessary retries) or false highs (bad outputs ship). Mitigated by judge model also being eval-harness-tested per [ADR-0110](0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md); regression tests for the evaluator itself.
.docs/decisions/0119-runtime-evaluator-pass-for-transform-abilities.md:247:- **`output_quality_score` amendment to [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md).** Tracked as a required follow-up amendment; must land before v1.4.1 evaluator infrastructure.
.docs/decisions/0119-runtime-evaluator-pass-for-transform-abilities.md:252:- Rubric tuning is a shared concern with [ADR-0110](0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md); expect some cycles of "lowered test-time threshold, didn't mean to lower runtime threshold too" in early v1.5.0. Address with rubric-change reviews.
.docs/decisions/0055-schedule-first-dashboard-layout.md:1:# ADR-0055: Schedule-First Dashboard Layout
.docs/decisions/0055-schedule-first-dashboard-layout.md:5:**Supersedes:** Layout portions of [ADR-0053](0053-dashboard-ux-redesign.md) (component decisions in 0053 remain accepted)
.docs/decisions/0055-schedule-first-dashboard-layout.md:9:ADR-0053 shipped the readiness strip, sidebar flip, greeting removal, and maxVisible improvements. But the daily briefing page still buries the meeting schedule behind 440-560px of preamble (date heading, focus callout, summary card, readiness strip, intelligence card). On a 13" MacBook, the schedule can be completely below the fold on a loaded day.
.docs/decisions/0096-glean-mode-local-footprint.md:1:# ADR-0096: Glean Mode Local Footprint
.docs/decisions/0096-glean-mode-local-footprint.md:5:**Extends:** ADR-0095 (Dual-Mode Context Architecture)
.docs/decisions/0096-glean-mode-local-footprint.md:9:When DailyOS operates in Glean mode (ADR-0095), Glean replaces several local data sources. This ADR documents what's stored locally in Glean mode, what's no longer stored, and how workspace file behavior changes.
.docs/decisions/0026-extension-architecture.md:1:# ADR-0026: Extension architecture with profile-activated modules
.docs/decisions/0088-people-relationship-network-intelligence.md:1:# ADR-0088: People Relationship Network Intelligence — Graph Model, Two-Layer Intelligence, Network Propagation
.docs/decisions/0088-people-relationship-network-intelligence.md:5:**Complements:** ADR-0087 (Entity Hierarchy Intelligence) — ADR-0087 explicitly excludes people from the hierarchy/rollup model because people's structural pattern is a relationship graph, not a tree. This ADR defines the people model.
.docs/decisions/0088-people-relationship-network-intelligence.md:6:**Extends:** ADR-0057 (Entity Intelligence Architecture) — two-file pattern for people already exists; this ADR adds the `network` section to that pattern.
.docs/decisions/0088-people-relationship-network-intelligence.md:7:**Uses:** ADR-0079 (Role Presets) — `entityModeDefault` and vocabulary fields shape how network intelligence is framed per role.
.docs/decisions/0088-people-relationship-network-intelligence.md:22:ADR-0087 explicitly deferred people from the hierarchy model: "People are explicitly excluded from this model — people don't have a tree hierarchy in DailyOS, they have a relationship network, which requires a different architectural approach not covered by this ADR." This ADR covers that approach.
.docs/decisions/0088-people-relationship-network-intelligence.md:32:- **Edge types:** `champion`, `executive_sponsor`, `decision_maker`, `technical_evaluator`, `blocker`, `peer`, `ally`, `detractor`, `collaborator`, `dependency`
.docs/decisions/0088-people-relationship-network-intelligence.md:34:- **Confidence:** Each edge carries a `confidence` score (0.0–1.0) derived from signal evidence or explicit user confirmation. An edge extracted from a transcript mention starts low confidence (~0.4). An edge confirmed by the user is set high (~0.9). Edges decay over time without reinforcement, following the same temporal decay model as signal confidence (ADR-0080).
.docs/decisions/0088-people-relationship-network-intelligence.md:59:Person intelligence already has a layer today (own signals). This ADR adds a second:
.docs/decisions/0088-people-relationship-network-intelligence.md:105:The existing signal bus (ADR-0080) handles cross-entity propagation. Person→account propagation already exists. This ADR adds person→person propagation:
.docs/decisions/0088-people-relationship-network-intelligence.md:107:**Trigger:** When a person emits a signal above a confidence threshold (default: 0.65), derived signals are generated for their relationship-graph neighbors.
.docs/decisions/0088-people-relationship-network-intelligence.md:119:| `decision_maker` | Yes (full multiplier) | Approval path changes are consequential |
.docs/decisions/0088-people-relationship-network-intelligence.md:127:**Propagation scope:** Person→person propagation does not cascade beyond 1 hop per cycle. A signal on Person A propagates to Person B (A's neighbor). It does NOT automatically propagate from B to C (B's neighbor) in the same cycle. This matches the ADR-0087 "direct children only" rule and uses the same loop-prevention mechanism: derived signals (source containing `propagation:network`) are not re-propagated.
.docs/decisions/0088-people-relationship-network-intelligence.md:145:The `network` section's AI-generated prose adapts to the active role preset's vocabulary field (ADR-0079). The relationship type names in the UI also adapt:
.docs/decisions/0088-people-relationship-network-intelligence.md:151:| `decision_maker` | Decision Maker | DACI Driver | Campaign Lead |
.docs/decisions/0088-people-relationship-network-intelligence.md:171:- Relationship type vocabulary adaptation requires a vocabulary map per preset. This is new data but follows the existing preset structure (ADR-0079) — add a `relationship_vocabulary` field to the preset type.
.docs/decisions/0088-people-relationship-network-intelligence.md:173:- Person→person propagation combined with the existing person→account propagation means a high-confidence signal on a person can now fan out to: their relationship graph neighbors AND their linked accounts. Confidence decay rates ensure this doesn't produce noise — a peer edge at 0.4 confidence propagating a 0.65 signal produces a 0.65 × 0.4 × 0.4 = ~0.10 derived signal, well below any enrichment threshold.
.docs/decisions/0010-simplified-sidebar.md:1:# ADR-0010: Focus, Week, Emails removed from sidebar
.docs/decisions/0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md:1:# ADR-0110: Evaluation Harness for Abilities
.docs/decisions/0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md:6:**Extends:** [ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md), [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md)  
.docs/decisions/0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md:7:**Depends on:** [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md), [ADR-0106](0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md), [ADR-0107](0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md)  
.docs/decisions/0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md:8:**Consumed by:** [ADR-0103](0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md) §9 (snapshot-diff evaluation), [ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) §13 (Phase 3 regression gate)
.docs/decisions/0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md:12:[ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) §10 Enforcement Phase 3 requires a regression gate before abilities become authoritative; Risk #5 declares the evaluation harness a non-negotiable co-dependency. [ADR-0103](0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md) §9 specifies snapshot-diff evaluation for maintenance abilities. [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md) §6 requires replay providers in `Evaluate` mode. [ADR-0106](0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md) §6 uses fingerprints to classify regression types.
.docs/decisions/0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md:14:Without a concrete harness, these requirements do not produce action. This ADR specifies fixture structure, scoring methodology, regression classification, CI integration, and fixture governance (retention, anonymization, source-aware lifecycle).
.docs/decisions/0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md:49:**Maintenance abilities** — snapshot-diff per [ADR-0103](0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md) §9. Compare `planned_mutations` against `expected_output.json.planned_mutations`. Diff on operation set, affected entity IDs, and parameter values. Thresholds: exact match or a configured tolerance (e.g., allow timestamp drift of ≤1s).
.docs/decisions/0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md:55:Per [ADR-0106](0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md) §6, fingerprints classify regressions:
.docs/decisions/0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md:134:Fixtures can contain PII and source-attributed content. Per [ADR-0098](0098-data-governance-source-aware-lifecycle.md) and [ADR-0107](0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md), fixtures must honor source lifecycle:
.docs/decisions/0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md:157:[ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) §13 Phase 3 cutover requires the regression gate to be active before the registry becomes authoritative. The gate's entry requirement is:
.docs/decisions/0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md:171:4. **Maintenance evaluation is tractable.** Snapshot-diff works because [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md) mode gating is structural.
.docs/decisions/0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md:173:6. **Fixture governance aligned with source lifecycle.** Anonymization and purge interoperate with [ADR-0107](0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md).
.docs/decisions/0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md:190:## Amendment — 2026-04-19 — §9 Harness-Stripping Fixtures (closes [ADR-0118](0118-dailyos-as-ai-harness-principles-and-residual-gaps.md) Gap B)
.docs/decisions/0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md:207:- `runtime_evaluator` — disable the ADR-0119 evaluator pass.
.docs/decisions/0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md:225:Stripped fixtures run on a schedule, not on every PR. Strip deltas are informational — they do not fail builds. A material finding (`strip_delta ≤ 0.02` across three quarterly cycles) opens a follow-on issue tagged `harness-retirement-candidate` for engineering review. Retirement is a conscious decision, not an automatic response.
.docs/decisions/0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md:245:- [ADR-0102: Abilities as the Runtime Contract](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) — Phase 3 regression gate depends on this harness.
.docs/decisions/0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md:246:- [ADR-0103: Maintenance Ability Safety Constraints](0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md) — §9 snapshot-diff evaluation consumes this harness.
.docs/decisions/0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md:247:- [ADR-0104: ExecutionMode and Mode-Aware Services](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md) — `Evaluate` mode enables hermetic evaluation; fixtures consumed via `ServiceContext::new_evaluate`.
.docs/decisions/0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md:248:- [ADR-0105: Provenance as First-Class Output](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) — Expected provenance is part of every fixture; attribution accuracy gauged via LLM self-eval.
.docs/decisions/0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md:249:- [ADR-0106: Prompt Fingerprinting and Provider Interface Extension](0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md) — Fingerprints drive regression classification in §3.
.docs/decisions/0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md:250:- [ADR-0107: Source Taxonomy Alignment](0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md) — Fixture lifecycle honors per-source revocation rules.
.docs/decisions/0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md:251:- [ADR-0098: Data Governance — Source-Aware Lifecycle](0098-data-governance-source-aware-lifecycle.md) — Anonymization and purge requirements.
.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:1:# ADR-0116: Tenant Control Plane Boundary — Metadata Only, Content Never
.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:6:**Extends:** [ADR-0092](0092-data-security-at-rest-and-operational-hardening.md)
.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:7:**Related:** [ADR-0099](0099-remote-first-server-canonical-architecture.md), [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md)
.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:12:DailyOS today is a single-user native macOS app. Every user's SQLite database is encrypted at rest with a key held in the macOS Keychain ([ADR-0092](0092-data-security-at-rest-and-operational-hardening.md)). There is no server-side component holding user content. This is a strong posture — it lets us tell a prospective customer "your content stays on your laptop" as a precise claim about *DailyOS's own server-side components* (which are none, in v1.4.0).
.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:14:**Precise wording required — 2026-04-20 update (outside voice finding #2).** "Stays on your laptop" is not accurate without qualification when Glean is the intelligence provider (per [ADR-0100](0100-glean-first-intelligence-architecture.md)). Glean's MCP chat tool receives prompts, returns completions, and sees the user's Glean-connected source data (Salesforce, Zendesk, Gong, Slack) in the Glean infrastructure the customer has already contracted with. That is not DailyOS control-plane — it is the user's pre-existing relationship with Glean — but it's also not "on the laptop."
.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:18:- **DailyOS's own server-side components** see no user content (not now; never without ADR amendment).
.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:19:- **DailyOS's local app** stores all user content encrypted on the user's device ([ADR-0092](0092-data-security-at-rest-and-operational-hardening.md)).
.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:20:- **Third-party intelligence providers** (Glean today, Anthropic/OpenAI/Ollama potentially via [ADR-0091](0091-intelligence-provider-abstraction.md)) see whatever the user's session sends them — prompts, partial context, completions. The user contracts with these providers independently. DailyOS routes to whichever provider the user has configured; DailyOS itself never stores the provider's responses beyond what lives locally.
.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:27:2. **Multi-user coordination.** Features like cross-analyst claim review ([ADR-0113](0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md) human claim sources), shared team intelligence, and cross-device sync require some server-side component that knows "these two sessions belong to the same org." That component is a control plane.
.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:33:This ADR establishes the boundary now, before the control plane exists, so that every future control-plane feature is measured against a hard rule. It also introduces the one substrate change that v1.4.0 must land so the boundary is physically enforceable later: a `DbKeyProvider` trait seam that lets enterprise BYOK slot in without a database schema migration.
.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:52:- Any row from any local table: `intelligence_claims`, `signal_events`, `meetings`, `accounts`, `persons`, everything.
.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:53:- Any ability output or Provenance envelope.
.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:57:- Any aggregate derived from user content (e.g., "this user has 47 claims about Acme").
.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:61:The one permitted aggregate crossing the line is operational telemetry — how many abilities invoked today, approximate token counts, error rates. These are counts with no entity references. Rendering decisions, content previews, "recent activity" feeds — none of these live on the control plane.
.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:63:**Permitted: user-opted-in anonymous aggregate telemetry (new category, 2026-04-20).** Per the outside voice finding that population-level metrics are needed to validate the harness bet, this ADR permits a specific additional class: telemetry the user explicitly opts into, containing only counts and category flags (never entity references, never content, never claim text), keyed on a non-reversible anonymized install ID. Examples of permitted metrics in this class:
.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:76:- Anything content-derived beyond counts (e.g., "claim body lengths" is forbidden even if anonymized; only "claim count" is permitted).
.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:88:- **ML convenience.** "Aggregate user corrections to improve prompts." No — user corrections stay on the user's device; improvements come from prompt rebaselining against evaluation fixtures ([ADR-0110](0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md)), not from content aggregation.
.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:91:Every future ADR that proposes a control-plane feature must cite this ADR and demonstrate that no content crosses. A review convention: if the server code can deserialize a user's entity, the design is wrong.
.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:106:Anything beyond this list requires an ADR amendment.
.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:134:Future implementations (v2.x and beyond, requiring ADR amendment to the registry):
.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:145:This is the single most important property this ADR establishes. Any design that would require a schema change to accommodate a different key provider is wrong and must be reworked.
.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:160:Under `ExecutionMode::Evaluate` ([ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md)), the DB is either an in-memory SQLite (fixture-loaded) or an unencrypted test database. The `DbKeyProvider` is either a `NoEncryption` stub or a `StaticKey` with a fixture-provided key. Tests do not hit the Keychain or any real control plane.
.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:173:The v1.4.0 shipment is the trait seam and nothing else. The boundary (§1) is a principle documented in this ADR; its structural enforcement arrives with the control plane in v2.x.
.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:182:- **Local-first promise is defensible.** We can point to this ADR when customers, reviewers, or future contributors ask how we enforce the boundary.
.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:188:- **"Metadata only" is sometimes debatable in practice.** "Approximate token counts" could theoretically leak patterns if an attacker had side-channel access. Accepted tradeoff — operational telemetry is essential; if specific fields become risky, they get removed. Every new metadata field added to the control plane must be reviewed against this ADR.
.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:189:- **Enterprise BYOK integration is future work with unknown surface area.** The trait seam does not solve KMS integration; it only ensures the shape of the call doesn't change. Real integration will require a v2.x ADR.
.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:190:- **A future contributor may be tempted to "just add a small cache" on the server.** This ADR is the thing reviewers cite to reject that PR. The boundary is defended by convention backed by this document.
.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:196:- [ADR-0092](0092-data-security-at-rest-and-operational-hardening.md) remains authoritative on at-rest encryption specifics; this ADR extends it with a forward-compatible seam.
.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:197:- [ADR-0099](0099-remote-first-server-canonical-architecture.md) — if/when a canonical server arrives, this ADR governs what it may do. The two ADRs are intended to compose.
.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:205:**Commercial corollary — how enterprise visibility gets served.** The anticipated enterprise objection ("leadership wants to see team activity") is answered structurally by the **publish framework** ([ADR-0117](0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md)), not by softening this boundary. The publish flow is a **user-initiated manual push** to an enterprise storage layer (human-readable or machine-readable), configured by the user per destination. The push or sync setup is performed by the user; it is not the control plane reading user data. Enterprise storage is the user's sink, not DailyOS's outbound channel.
.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:207:This means [ADR-0117](0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md) is now strategically load-bearing for DailyOS's enterprise commercial story. It must support:
.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:214:A follow-on decision before the first enterprise conversation: what destinations ship in v1.4.2's first publish ability beyond P2. The publish framework in [ADR-0117](0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md) R1.11 already supports this extension; enterprise destination set is a scope question, not an architectural one.
.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:224:That second path is open architecture. Filed as [ADR-0121](0121-team-intelligence-architecture.md) (Open) as a forward-looking placeholder with the problem framing and option space. Implementation deferred; decision deferred. Acknowledged as strategic risk in the v1.4.0 strategy doc signature block.
.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:230:- Publish framework ([ADR-0117](0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md)) remains as the reporting/export channel.
.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:234:- ADR-0117's "Strategic Elevation" section is corrected to remove the overclaim that publish answers the full enterprise question (separate amendment to ADR-0117).
.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:236:- The next commercial touchpoint asking for team intelligence triggers [ADR-0121](0121-team-intelligence-architecture.md) work, not an ADR-0116 softening.
.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:242:Adversarial review + reference pass confirmed the boundary principle is sound but flagged that the trait seam's "zero-impact" claim is wrong, the signature in the ADR doesn't match current code, and revocation is hand-waved for long-running desktop sessions.
.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:339:Codex flagged: key version, wrap-key ref, rotation state, rekey progress all need storage. The ADR's "no schema change ever" was too strong.
.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:366:Codex flagged: [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) provenance includes actor metadata; [ADR-0117](0117-publish-boundary-pencil-and-pen.md) publish records include destination refs. This ADR didn't classify whether those are content, control-plane metadata, or local-only metadata.
.docs/decisions/0116-tenant-control-plane-boundary.md:379:- `DbKeyProviderAsync` trait — defined in this ADR but not implemented until a user shows up.
.docs/decisions/0049-eliminate-python-runtime.md:1:# ADR-0049: Eliminate Python runtime dependency
.docs/decisions/0049-eliminate-python-runtime.md:19:- ADR-0042 (I38) replaced `deliver_today.py` entirely with `workflow/deliver.rs`
.docs/decisions/0049-eliminate-python-runtime.md:63:**Supersedes:** ADR-0006 (determinism boundary) described Python as the Phase 1/3 executor. Phase 3 was already eliminated by ADR-0042. This eliminates Phase 1, making the three-phase pattern fully Rust-native. The conceptual boundary (fetch → enrich → deliver) remains; only the implementation language changes.
.docs/decisions/0062-briefing-artifacts-vs-live-queries.md:1:# ADR-0062: Briefing Artifacts vs. Live Queries — Data Paradigm Boundary
.docs/decisions/0062-briefing-artifacts-vs-live-queries.md:102:**Supersedes:** I180's proposal to enrich schedule.json with computed fields. I180 is reframed: the architectural decision is this ADR; the implementation work is I178 (available time query) and I179 (action feasibility query).
.docs/decisions/0016-defer-post-meeting-to-phase-3.md:1:# ADR-0016: Defer post-meeting capture to Phase 3
