.docs/decisions/0111-surface-independent-ability-invocation.md:1:# ADR-0111: Surface-Independent Ability Invocation
.docs/decisions/0111-surface-independent-ability-invocation.md:6:**Extends:** [ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md), [ADR-0027](0027-mcp-dual-mode.md)  
.docs/decisions/0111-surface-independent-ability-invocation.md:7:**Depends on:** [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md), [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md), [ADR-0108](0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md)
.docs/decisions/0111-surface-independent-ability-invocation.md:11:[ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) §7 specifies the ability registry and declares that surfaces invoke abilities through a single path. §10 Phase 2 requires migrating app Tauri commands and MCP sidecar handlers to wrap ability invocations. The existing MCP sidecar ([ADR-0027](0027-mcp-dual-mode.md)) has its own query logic and data access path; migrating it requires concrete binding rules.
.docs/decisions/0111-surface-independent-ability-invocation.md:13:This ADR specifies the exact binding pattern for Tauri commands and MCP tools, how abilities are generated into both surfaces from the registry, and how surfaces construct `AbilityContext` with mode, actor, and provenance renderer wiring.
.docs/decisions/0111-surface-independent-ability-invocation.md:26:| Evaluation harness | `System` | `Evaluate` | `EvalAbilityBridge` per [ADR-0110](0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md) |
.docs/decisions/0111-surface-independent-ability-invocation.md:65:6. Renders provenance via [ADR-0108](0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md)'s `render_provenance_for(prov, User, TauriApp)`.
.docs/decisions/0111-surface-independent-ability-invocation.md:93:7. Renders provenance via [ADR-0108](0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md)'s `render_provenance_for(prov, Agent, McpTool)`.
.docs/decisions/0111-surface-independent-ability-invocation.md:96:**No hand-written MCP tool handlers.** Every MCP tool is an ability. Pre-existing MCP tools from [ADR-0027](0027-mcp-dual-mode.md) that do not correspond to an ability are either migrated to an ability or removed during Phase 3 cutover.
.docs/decisions/0111-surface-independent-ability-invocation.md:127:Scheduled maintenance runs in `Live` mode with `Actor::System`. The worker is the only code path that invokes `global` blast radius maintenance without user confirmation (per [ADR-0103](0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md) §7).
.docs/decisions/0111-surface-independent-ability-invocation.md:131:[ADR-0110](0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md) defines the harness; this ADR specifies how it binds into the same registry:
.docs/decisions/0111-surface-independent-ability-invocation.md:155:- MCP server registers ability-derived tools alongside existing hand-written tools (both paths active).
.docs/decisions/0111-surface-independent-ability-invocation.md:178:6. **Provenance rendering integrated into bridges.** Every response carries appropriately-rendered provenance per [ADR-0108](0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md).
.docs/decisions/0111-surface-independent-ability-invocation.md:183:2. **MCP migration is non-trivial.** Existing MCP tools from [ADR-0027](0027-mcp-dual-mode.md) must be refactored into abilities or retired.
.docs/decisions/0111-surface-independent-ability-invocation.md:196:- [ADR-0102: Abilities as the Runtime Contract](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) — Registry and §7 binding rules.
.docs/decisions/0111-surface-independent-ability-invocation.md:197:- [ADR-0027: MCP Dual-Mode](0027-mcp-dual-mode.md) — Existing MCP sidecar; Phase 3 retires its hand-written tools in favor of registry-derived ones.
.docs/decisions/0111-surface-independent-ability-invocation.md:198:- [ADR-0104: ExecutionMode and Mode-Aware Services](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md) — Mode is set by surfaces here.
.docs/decisions/0111-surface-independent-ability-invocation.md:199:- [ADR-0108: Provenance Rendering and Privacy](0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md) — Bridges call the renderer on return.
.docs/decisions/0111-surface-independent-ability-invocation.md:200:- [ADR-0110: Evaluation Harness for Abilities](0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md) — Eval bridge is a surface using the same pattern.
.docs/decisions/0111-surface-independent-ability-invocation.md:201:- [ADR-0103: Maintenance Ability Safety Constraints](0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md) — Worker is the only path for scheduled `global` maintenance.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:1:# ADR-0102: Abilities as the Runtime Contract
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:6:**Extends:** [ADR-0101](0101-service-boundary-enforcement.md), [ADR-0091](0091-intelligence-provider-abstraction.md), [ADR-0082](0082-entity-generic-prep-pipeline.md)  
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:7:**Related:** [ADR-0080](0080-signal-intelligence-architecture.md), [ADR-0097](0097-account-health-scoring-architecture.md), [ADR-0098](0098-data-governance-source-aware-lifecycle.md), [ADR-0100](0100-glean-first-intelligence-architecture.md)
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:20:The substrate ADRs — services ([ADR-0101](0101-service-boundary-enforcement.md)), signals ([ADR-0080](0080-signal-intelligence-architecture.md)), entities ([ADR-0082](0082-entity-generic-prep-pipeline.md)), providers ([ADR-0091](0091-intelligence-provider-abstraction.md)), data governance ([ADR-0098](0098-data-governance-source-aware-lifecycle.md)), Glean-first retrieval ([ADR-0100](0100-glean-first-intelligence-architecture.md)) — establish how state is mutated, how signals flow, how entities are modeled, and how intelligence is retrieved. None of them establishes how a **product capability** is shaped, named, versioned, or exposed. That layer is missing.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:22:This ADR introduces it.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:34:- Returns a typed output wrapped in `AbilityOutput<T>`, which carries the domain output alongside a mandatory provenance envelope. Provenance shape is specified in [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md). The domain output itself does not re-embed provenance.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:41:- A bare CRUD function (those belong in `services/`, per [ADR-0101](0101-service-boundary-enforcement.md)).
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:53:├── provenance.rs               # Provenance types (detail in ADR-0105)
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:74:Each ability is a single module — typically one file, but a directory is permitted for abilities large enough to warrant submodules (e.g., `prepare_meeting/` containing `mod.rs`, `prompts.rs`, `synthesis.rs`, `tests.rs`). The module has one public entry point (the ability function) and opaque internals. Eval fixtures live under `src-tauri/tests/abilities/{ability_name}/` (detail in [ADR-0107](0107-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md)).
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:85:| **Maintenance** | Mutates internal state through services. Subject to [ADR-0103](0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md). | Sometimes | `refresh_entity_state`, `reconcile_signals` |
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:90:- **Transform** abilities are eval-gated on output quality (see [ADR-0107](0107-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md)). Their outputs are marked untrusted by default (§10); they cannot authorize mutation on their own, but they can participate in mutation-authorizing composition when paired with a trust signal (user confirmation, policy pre-authorization, or schema-constrained value).
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:91:- **Publish** abilities require a `ConfirmationToken` from the invoking surface. A maintenance ability cannot call a publish ability unless its registry policy explicitly grants `may_publish = true`, which requires an ADR amendment.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:92:- **Maintenance** abilities are bound by [ADR-0103](0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md). Contributors writing a maintenance ability MUST read ADR-0103; the constraints are non-optional.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:116:`AbilityContext` is the single dependency entry point for abilities, analogous to `ServiceContext` ([ADR-0101](0101-service-boundary-enforcement.md)) but operating one layer higher. `ServiceContext` itself is amended to carry `ExecutionMode` in [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md), which is a prerequisite to this ADR.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:120:    pub services: &'a ServiceContext<'a>,      // Carries DB, signals, queue, mode, clock, RNG — see ADR-0104
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:121:    pub intelligence: &'a dyn IntelligenceProvider,  // ADR-0091; scope expansion noted in §12
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:122:    pub user: &'a UserEntity,                   // ADR-0089
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:132:- Abilities never instantiate an LLM provider. They receive one via `ctx.intelligence`, which is replayable in `Simulate`/`Evaluate` modes per [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md) §6.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:134:- Abilities never access Glean directly. Glean access is provided through mode-aware services at `ctx.services.external.glean.*` per [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md) §2, which route to the live Glean client in `Live` mode and to replay fixtures in `Simulate`/`Evaluate`. A direct `GleanClient` reference on `AbilityContext` would bypass mode-aware external-call gating and is forbidden.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:135:- Execution mode lives on `ServiceContext`, not `AbilityContext`. Services enforce mode at the mutation boundary; abilities do not need to remember it. See [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md).
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:168:    pub provenance: Provenance,           // Canonical location — see ADR-0105
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:176:- Provenance lives on `AbilityOutput<T>`, exactly one place. Domain output types never re-declare a `provenance` field. Reviewers reject such declarations at registration.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:177:- Field-level provenance attribution (how `suggested_outcomes[0]` ties back to specific signals) is specified in [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md). This ADR requires that the mechanism exist; 0105 defines the shape.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:193:    // AbilityPolicy fields — canonical schema used across all ADRs:
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:209:The `AbilityPolicy` schema is canonical across all ADRs:
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:220:ADR-0103 §6 uses these exact field names. There is no `actor_policy` shorthand — the macro attribute names match the struct fields one-to-one.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:247:- Eval harness discovery ([ADR-0107](0107-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md)).
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:278:4. **Prompt changes are not ability version changes.** Prompt iteration is owned by the ability and evaluated per [ADR-0107](0107-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md). A prompt regression is caught by eval, not by version.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:293:Services ([ADR-0101](0101-service-boundary-enforcement.md)) do not call abilities. If a service needs synthesized context, the correct pattern is for the caller to invoke an ability and pass its output to the service.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:296:A Transform may invoke Read. A Publish may invoke Transform and Read. Maintenance may invoke Read and Transform. **Maintenance may NOT invoke Publish unless `may_publish = true` is explicitly declared in the registry and justified by ADR amendment** — otherwise scheduled jobs could publish externally without user confirmation. Composition cycles are rejected at registration time (see §11).
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:299:Provenance lives exactly once, on the wrapper. Domain output types never re-declare it. The mechanism is what makes depth explainable and quality measurable. Shape specified in [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md).
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:302:Any ability in the Maintenance category is further bound by [ADR-0103](0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md). A maintenance ability that does not honor those constraints is non-conforming regardless of its compliance with Rules 1–5.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:321:- Transform outputs carry a `trust: Trust::Untrusted` marker in their provenance ([ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md)).
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:345:**Nested transactions are forbidden.** A child ability that calls `with_transaction_async` while one is already active returns `Error::NestedTransactionsForbidden` per [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md) §4. Child abilities that need transactional writes rely on the parent's transaction or are refactored to be invoked outside a transactional scope.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:347:**Publish inside a transaction is forbidden.** A Publish ability called inside a `with_transaction_async` scope fails at registration time — the registry flags the composition attempt. DailyOS does not cross transactional and external-mutation boundaries within a single call stack. If a maintenance ability needs to publish after its writes, it uses the outbox pattern specified in [ADR-0103](0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md) §2: local writes inside the transaction, external call outside it with idempotency key.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:349:ADR-0101's `with_transaction` is synchronous. [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md) introduces `with_transaction_async` for async composition. Maintenance abilities that require transactional composition use the async variant. Transform and Read abilities do not open transactions.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:351:#### 11.3 Provenance Merging
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:353:When ability A invokes ability B, A's provenance envelope includes B's provenance as a nested child. The full provenance tree shows the composition. Deduplication rules and merge semantics specified in [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md).
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:361:Two substrate ADRs constrain how abilities consume intelligence: [ADR-0091](0091-intelligence-provider-abstraction.md) (pluggable LLM providers) and [ADR-0100](0100-glean-first-intelligence-architecture.md) (Glean is the primary intelligence engine when connected).
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:365:ADR-0091 introduced `IntelligenceProvider` for the intel queue's LLM calls. This ADR expands its use to be the canonical LLM dependency for all Transform abilities. The scope expansion is intentional and supersedes ADR-0091's implicit limit to the intel queue. `AbilityContext.intelligence` is the only LLM entry point available to abilities; direct PTY construction or provider instantiation within an ability is forbidden. Pre-existing PTY call sites that ADR-0091 deliberately did not migrate remain permitted for non-ability code paths (ingestion, background processors) but are not accessible to abilities.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:376:When Glean is not connected, Read abilities fall back to local PTY-based computation per [ADR-0100](0100-glean-first-intelligence-architecture.md). The Transform ability is unchanged — it composes whatever the Read returned. This keeps abilities engine-agnostic while preserving the Glean-first posture.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:381:[ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md) is accepted and implemented. `ServiceContext` carries `ExecutionMode`. Mutation functions honor the mode. Without this, §5 and §11.2 of this ADR are not enforceable.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:384:[ADR-0103](0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md) through [ADR-0109](0109-migration-strategy.md) land as Proposed. Initial ability catalog drafted. Eval harness scaffolding in place per [ADR-0107](0107-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md). No implementation churn yet.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:387:`prepare_meeting`, `get_daily_readiness`, `get_entity_context`, `list_open_loops`, `detect_risk_shift` migrate behind abilities. App Tauri commands become thin wrappers. MCP sidecar query handlers become thin wrappers over the same abilities. Parallel-run validation per [ADR-0109](0109-migration-strategy.md).
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:402:4. Parallel-run validation compares old and new outputs on live traffic until confidence is sufficient (detail in [ADR-0109](0109-migration-strategy.md)).
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:405:Services per [ADR-0101](0101-service-boundary-enforcement.md) are untouched except for the mode-aware amendment in [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md). The abilities layer sits above services, not beside them.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:415:5. **Prompt iteration becomes disciplined.** Prompts are owned by abilities and evaluated per [ADR-0107](0107-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md). Prompt changes ship with eval deltas.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:425:3. **Two implementations during migration.** Commands call both old paths and new abilities until cutover. Managed per [ADR-0109](0109-migration-strategy.md) but genuinely more work in the intermediate state.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:427:5. **Provenance is not free.** Every ability must attach attribution to every output field. This is a design discipline that takes effort to apply consistently.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:435:5. **Eval harness dependency.** Abilities only deliver their promised benefit if evaluated. Without [ADR-0107](0107-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md) landing alongside this one, the discipline decays and quality becomes unmeasurable again. The eval harness is non-negotiable co-dependency.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:436:6. **Migration stall.** Phase 2 requires holding other capability-level feature work steady for the duration. If product pressure breaks this, the refactor stalls half-complete. Mitigation: v1.4.0 is a dedicated version with no competing feature scope, per the roadmap decision.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:441:- [ADR-0101: Service Boundary Enforcement](0101-service-boundary-enforcement.md) — Services remain the mutation boundary. Abilities compose services; they do not replace them. Amended by [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md) for mode awareness.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:442:- [ADR-0091: IntelligenceProvider Abstraction](0091-intelligence-provider-abstraction.md) — Abilities consume `IntelligenceProvider` via `AbilityContext`. Scope is expanded by this ADR from the intel queue to all Transform abilities; see §12.1.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:443:- [ADR-0082: Entity-Generic Prep Pipeline](0082-entity-generic-prep-pipeline.md) — The entity model is the substrate on which abilities operate. `get_entity_context` generalizes the prep pipeline.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:444:- [ADR-0080: Signal Intelligence Architecture](0080-signal-intelligence-architecture.md) — Signals are the primary input to transform abilities. Provenance references signal IDs.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:445:- [ADR-0098: Data Governance — Source-Aware Lifecycle](0098-data-governance-source-aware-lifecycle.md) — Data source tagging flows through into ability provenance.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:446:- [ADR-0100: Glean-First Intelligence Architecture](0100-glean-first-intelligence-architecture.md) — Abilities orchestrate around Glean; they do not replace its computation. See §12.2.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:447:- [ADR-0089: User Entity and Professional Context](0089-user-entity-and-professional-context.md) — `AbilityContext.user` carries professional context that shapes synthesis.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:448:- **[ADR-0103: Maintenance Ability Safety Constraints](0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md)** — Specifies the eleven structural guards that bind maintenance abilities. Co-dependency with this ADR; maintenance abilities without these constraints are non-conforming.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:449:- **[ADR-0104: ExecutionMode and Mode-Aware Services](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md)** — Prerequisite. Amends ADR-0101 to make `ServiceContext` mode-aware. Required for §5, §11.2, and async transaction support.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:450:- **ADR-0105 (forthcoming): Provenance as First-Class Output** — Specifies the provenance shape, field-level attribution, trust markers, merge semantics, and rendering contract referenced throughout this ADR.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:451:- **ADR-0106 (forthcoming): Temporal Primitives in the Entity Graph** — Defines the trajectory types (engagement curves, role progressions, health trajectory) that abilities consume.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:452:- **ADR-0107 (forthcoming): Evaluation Harness for Abilities** — Defines how abilities are scored, gated, and regressed. Co-dependency per Risk #5.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:453:- **ADR-0108 (forthcoming): Surface-Independent Ability Invocation** — Specifies MCP and Tauri binding rules concretely (which follows from §7.3).
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:454:- **ADR-0109 (forthcoming): Migration Strategy — Parallel Run and Cutover** — Specifies the per-capability migration process, parallel-run validation, and cutover criteria.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:464:The original ADR defines `AbilityResult<T> = Result<AbilityOutput<T>, AbilityError>` but does not specify how abilities handle partial failure, composition failure, or soft degradation. The distinction matters because surfaces render different things based on outcome class.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:468:1. **Hard error** — ability returns `Err(AbilityError::...)`. Caller sees no output. Surface decides user-visible message per [ADR-0108](0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md). No partial data leaks.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:470:2. **Soft degradation** — ability returns `Ok(AbilityOutput<T>)` with `Provenance::warnings` populated. Output is usable; warnings tell the surface that parts were degraded. Examples: child Read timed out so context is thin; a stale source was included flagged as stale; LLM output anomaly was detected but the output itself still validated.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:474:**Forbidden:** an ability silently logs an anomaly and returns "normal" output. Every anomaly is either hard-error-surfaced, warning-surfaced, or not worth recording. The log-and-proceed path that [ADR-0118](0118-dailyos-as-ai-harness-principles-and-residual-gaps.md)'s critique flagged is explicitly prohibited.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:483:The `warnings[]` field on Provenance is the canonical record of "what went soft." Surfaces render it per [ADR-0108](0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md).
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:487:The ADR contract is designed for shipping abilities. It is heavy for prototyping. At AI-native velocity, trying a new ability shape should be hours, not days-of-ADR.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:491:- Provenance envelope requirement: minimal. A bare `Provenance::experimental()` constructor is permitted with only `invocation_id` + `produced_at` populated.
.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:498:Rationale: exploration needs a fast path. Discipline comes from graduation (promotion requires full ADR compliance), not from blocking experimentation entirely. The one-cycle lifespan prevents "experimental" from becoming a permanent escape hatch.
.docs/decisions/0024-email-ai-triage-not-client.md:1:# ADR-0024: Email = AI triage, not email client
.docs/decisions/0124-longitudinal-topic-threading.md:1:# ADR-0124: Longitudinal Topic Threading — Substrate Allowance and Deferred Decision
.docs/decisions/0124-longitudinal-topic-threading.md:3:**Status:** Accepted (substrate allowance for v1.4.0 spine; design decision deferred to v1.4.2)
.docs/decisions/0124-longitudinal-topic-threading.md:5:**Target:** v1.4.0 substrate (schema + envelope allowance) / v1.4.2 (decision + implementation)
.docs/decisions/0124-longitudinal-topic-threading.md:6:**Extends:** [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) (SubjectAttribution amendment), [ADR-0113](0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md) (claims)
.docs/decisions/0124-longitudinal-topic-threading.md:15:- Per-`(entity_id, claim_type, field_path)` claim history ([ADR-0113](0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md) R1.6).
.docs/decisions/0124-longitudinal-topic-threading.md:16:- Multi-subject claims spanning Account / Project / Person / Meeting ([ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) SubjectAttribution amendment).
.docs/decisions/0124-longitudinal-topic-threading.md:17:- Temporal primitives `EngagementCurve` and `RoleProgression` ([ADR-0109](0109-temporal-primitives-in-the-entity-graph.md), DOS-215).
.docs/decisions/0124-longitudinal-topic-threading.md:21:Two solution shapes are plausible (detailed in §3 below). The decision between them needs design work and pilot data that is not v1.4.0 spine scope. But choosing later is expensive if the substrate has no place to put a thread reference. This ADR resolves that mismatch: ship the substrate allowance now, defer the meaning to v1.4.2.
