.docs/decisions/0083-product-vocabulary.md:1:# ADR-0083: Product Vocabulary — User-Facing Language Standard
.docs/decisions/0083-product-vocabulary.md:7:**Related:** ADR-0073 (Editorial design language), ADR-0076 (Brand identity), ADR-0081 (Event-driven meeting intelligence), I329 (Intelligence quality indicators)
.docs/decisions/0083-product-vocabulary.md:13:DailyOS has been built at speed. The codebase, the ADRs, the UI copy, and the backlog all use language that was convenient for the builders — "entity," "enrichment," "signal," "intelligence," "prep." These terms are precise for the system's internals but meaningless or confusing for the person using the app.
.docs/decisions/0083-product-vocabulary.md:17:1. **System vocabulary** — what developers and ADRs use to describe architecture. Precise, abstract, composable.
.docs/decisions/0083-product-vocabulary.md:22:The editorial design language (ADR-0073) gave DailyOS a visual identity — serif headlines, material palette, magazine layout. This ADR gives it a verbal identity.
.docs/decisions/0083-product-vocabulary.md:34:- "Account" used for both external customers and internal teams (ADR-0070)
.docs/decisions/0083-product-vocabulary.md:44:**System vocabulary** lives in code, ADRs, and developer documentation. It is precise and abstract. `entity`, `signal`, `enrichment`, `intelligence`, `prep`, `resolution` — all fine here.
.docs/decisions/0083-product-vocabulary.md:95:ADR-0081 defines four quality levels: Sparse, Developing, Ready, Fresh. These are system-accurate but user-unfriendly. "Sparse" sounds like an error. "Developing" sounds like it's the system's problem.
.docs/decisions/0083-product-vocabulary.md:107:**The "new updates" indicator** (blue dot in ADR-0081) becomes simply a dot with no label. Its presence says "something changed." Tapping reveals what.
.docs/decisions/0083-product-vocabulary.md:113:Beyond individual terms, the product has a voice. The editorial design language (ADR-0073) established the visual voice: restrained, confident, magazine-editorial. The verbal voice should match:
.docs/decisions/0083-product-vocabulary.md:128:- **ADR language.** ADRs continue using system vocabulary. They're developer documents.
.docs/decisions/0083-product-vocabulary.md:138:This ADR is a reference document, not an implementation plan. The implementation is a UI copy audit (see I341) that systematically finds every user-visible string using system vocabulary and translates it.
.docs/decisions/0083-product-vocabulary.md:163:- **Design decisions get easier.** When writing new UI copy, the translation table is the reference. No more ad-hoc decisions about whether to say "intelligence" or "context."
.docs/decisions/0083-product-vocabulary.md:168:- **Existing documentation references system terms.** Users reading ADRs or changelogs will see different vocabulary than the app. Acceptable — these are developer documents.
.docs/decisions/0083-product-vocabulary.md:179:- ADR-0073: Editorial design language (visual voice)
.docs/decisions/0083-product-vocabulary.md:180:- ADR-0076: Brand identity (material palette, asterisk mark)
.docs/decisions/0083-product-vocabulary.md:181:- ADR-0081: Event-driven meeting intelligence (quality indicators, surface roles)
.docs/decisions/0083-product-vocabulary.md:183:- I341: Product vocabulary audit (the implementation of this ADR)
