.docs/decisions/0061-calendar-event-id-as-meeting-key.md:9:Meetings are identified throughout the system by a derived slug: `make_meeting_id(title, start_time, type)` → e.g., `1030-customer-acme-weekly-sync`. This slug is:
.docs/decisions/0061-calendar-event-id-as-meeting-key.md:21:1. `deliver_schedule` sets `hasPrep: true` and generates a prep filename using one code path. `deliver_preps` generates the actual file using a different code path. When the derived meeting IDs diverge, the "View Prep" button links to a file that doesn't exist.
.docs/decisions/0061-calendar-event-id-as-meeting-key.md:39:5. **`make_meeting_id` retained for non-calendar meetings.** Manually created meetings or imported meetings without calendar IDs still need a derived ID. `make_meeting_id` becomes the fallback, not the default.
.docs/decisions/0061-calendar-event-id-as-meeting-key.md:53:- Prep file lookup is deterministic — `deliver_schedule` and `deliver_preps` always agree on the filename because they use the same event ID, not a derived slug.
.docs/decisions/0042-per-operation-pipelines.md:1:# ADR-0042: Per-operation pipelines with progressive delivery
.docs/decisions/0042-per-operation-pipelines.md:12:ADR-0030 decomposed Phase 1 into atomic operations (`ops/`). Phase 2 (one big Claude Code invocation) and Phase 3 (one big deliver script) remain monolithic bottlenecks.
.docs/decisions/0042-per-operation-pipelines.md:38:**ADR-0006 is preserved, not superseded.** The determinism boundary still holds: deterministic phases wrap each AI call. The change is granularity — N small deterministic-AI-deterministic pipelines instead of one large one.
.docs/decisions/0042-per-operation-pipelines.md:40:**Feature toggles (ADR-0039) gate operations at the orchestrator level.** If Gmail isn't connected, `email:fetch` is skipped entirely — but calendar and actions still run and deliver. This prevents incomplete runs at the source.
