.docs/decisions/0047-entity-dashboard-architecture.md:18:ADR-0004 established JSON for machines, markdown for humans. ADR-0018 established markdown as source of truth, SQLite as disposable cache. ADR-0028 established JSON-first schemas with markdown generated from JSON. These decisions are sound but create a tension for entity dashboards: who owns what when multiple tools can write?
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:32:- A maintenance ability invoked in `ExecutionMode::Evaluate` writes to real tables because the mode is advisory rather than enforced.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:101:This ADR relies on [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md) which makes `ExecutionMode` load-bearing in the service layer. The three modes (`Live`, `Simulate`, `Evaluate`) have precise and non-overlapping semantics, summarized below. Maintenance abilities additionally honor a `dry_run` input flag that is orthogonal to mode. All four operational paths rely on a common **plan/apply split**: abilities compute a planned mutation set via non-mutating planning calls, then either apply the plan (Live, live-apply phase) or short-circuit before application (Simulate, Evaluate, or Live+dry_run).
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:103:| Concern | Live | Simulate | Evaluate | Live + dry_run=true |
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:115:- **`Simulate` vs `Evaluate`.** Both reject mutations and signals. `Evaluate` additionally supplies a deterministic clock, seeded RNG, and replay providers (per [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md) §6) so snapshot-diff scoring is stable across runs. `Simulate` is a developer-facing "let me try this without side effects" mode where determinism is not guaranteed and tracer output is verbose but not normalized.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:116:- **`dry_run` vs `Simulate`.** `dry_run` runs in `Live` mode with real providers and real reads; the ability short-circuits before the apply phase. Use it for user-initiated "show me what you would do" flows where synthesis quality must match production. `Simulate` runs with stubbed providers and replay fixtures — use it for offline developer work where real API costs or latency are unwanted.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:117:- **Audit contract resolution.** `MaintenanceIntent` and `MaintenanceOutcome` (§8) are operational audit records, not signals. In `Live` mode, they are persisted to a dedicated `maintenance_audit` table, not the signal bus. In `Simulate` and `Evaluate` modes, they are captured to the tracer. `Live + dry_run=true` writes them to the audit table — the invocation happened; the mutations were chosen to be skipped.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:121:- Every maintenance ability supports `dry_run: bool` in its input. CI-generated conformance tests verify both `Live + dry_run=true` and a full `Evaluate` run produce identical `planned_mutations`.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:141:    allowed_modes = [Live, Simulate, Evaluate],
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:211:    pub allowed_modes: Vec<ExecutionMode>,
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:259:    pub mode: ExecutionMode,
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:298:   - `Some(ModeNonLive)` — Simulate or Evaluate; apply was skipped by plan/apply discipline
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:304:- **Simulate and Evaluate:** records captured to `AbilityTracer` only, never persisted. This honors the mode contract (no side effects in Simulate/Evaluate) while still producing a full trace for debugging or evaluation scoring.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:327:- **Run:** invoke the ability in `ExecutionMode::Evaluate` with `dry_run = true`.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:331:This is more expensive to set up than transform-ability eval but becomes tractable once §3 (ExecutionMode enforcement and dry-run contract) is in place. [ADR-0107](0107-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md) specifies the harness mechanics and fixture governance.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:400:2. **Service layer takes on mode-checking responsibility.** Every mutation function honors `ExecutionMode` (per [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md)). Touches a lot of code but is mechanical.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:416:9. **Mode contract violations.** A maintenance ability or a composed sub-ability accidentally writes in Simulate/Evaluate mode. Mitigation: enforcement lives in the service layer ([ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md)) — abilities cannot bypass it.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:425:- **[ADR-0104: ExecutionMode and Mode-Aware Services](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md)** — Prerequisite. Specifies how `ExecutionMode` flows through `ServiceContext`, how mutation functions honor it, and `with_transaction_async` primitive.
.docs/decisions/0126-memory-substrate-invariants.md:10:The v1.4.0 claim substrate (DOS-7 `intelligence_claims`, DOS-5 Trust Compiler, DOS-10 freshness decay, DOS-211 Provenance, DOS-212 DataSource taxonomy, DOS-213 prompt fingerprinting, DOS-263 invalidation_jobs, DOS-265 claim_edges, DOS-280 canonicalization) is large, interlocking, and built by multiple contributors over multiple releases. Without a unifying set of invariants, each contributor independently makes small decisions that drift apart over time. The result: two code paths that could in principle do the same thing end up doing subtly different things, ghost-resurrection bugs re-emerge after they were fixed, and every new feature ends up re-solving the "how do I store this?" problem.
.docs/decisions/0126-memory-substrate-invariants.md:38:`DELETE FROM intelligence_claims` is forbidden outside user-data-deletion flows (GDPR/CCPA). Same for `claim_corroborations` and `claim_contradictions`.
.docs/decisions/0126-memory-substrate-invariants.md:42:Enforcement: CI lint grep for `DELETE FROM intelligence_claims|claim_corroborations|claim_contradictions` outside the allowed paths.
.docs/decisions/0126-memory-substrate-invariants.md:48:Explicit user judgment (correction, tombstone, confirmation) flows through `claim_feedback` per ADR-0123 typed feedback semantics. Explicit judgment affects trust via DOS-5's `fb` factor.
.docs/decisions/0126-memory-substrate-invariants.md:90:- Feedback events (correction, confirmation, tombstone) → `claim_feedback` (ADR-0123 typed) → DOS-5's `fb` factor → trust.
.docs/decisions/0126-memory-substrate-invariants.md:133:- New features that would be easy to slam into a single column on `intelligence_claims` have to route through the right substrate layer. Some features will take slightly longer to ship as a result; the tradeoff is that the substrate doesn't decay over time.
.docs/decisions/0126-memory-substrate-invariants.md:148:- DOS-7 (intelligence_claims — amendments A, B, C, D, E trace back here).
