    89	Nine variants, each mapping to a distinct triple of (claim state, source weight, agent ledger). No 5-point scale. No yes/no.
    90	
    91	### 2. `ClaimFeedback` row shape (closed)
    92	
    93	```rust
    94	pub struct ClaimFeedback {
    95	    pub id: ClaimFeedbackId,
    96	    pub claim_id: ClaimId,
    97	    pub subject: SubjectRef,                  // From ADR-0105 SubjectAttribution amendment
    98	    pub action: FeedbackAction,
    99	    pub note: Option<String>,
   100	    pub created_by: ClaimActor,               // Almost always User in v1.4.0; future agent-to-agent feedback
   101	    pub created_at: DateTime<Utc>,
   102	    pub applied_at: Option<DateTime<Utc>>,    // Set by Trust Compiler when consumed
   103	}
   104	```
   105	
   106	Stored in `claim_feedback` table. **Append-only.** The Trust Compiler reads the latest unconsumed rows on each scoring pass and writes `applied_at` after consuming. Re-application is idempotent: a row with `applied_at IS NOT NULL` is skipped.
   107	
   108	### 3. The 7 open decisions, closed
   109	
   110	| # | Question | Answer | Where it lives |
   111	|---|---|---|---|
   112	| 1 | Which feedback actions are first-class in v1.4.0 vs. v1.4.2? | All nine variants are first-class in the **enum and DB row** in v1.4.0 (DOS-294). Inline UI surfaces in DOS-8 expose six (`ConfirmCurrent`, `MarkOutdated`, `MarkFalse`, `WrongSubject`, `WrongSource`, `CannotVerify`) behind a low/medium-trust gate. The remaining three (`NeedsNuance`, `SurfaceInappropriate`, `NotRelevantHere`) ship behind an overflow menu in v1.4.2. | §1; UI work split between DOS-8 (v1.4.0) and v1.4.2 |
   113	| 2 | Does `CannotVerify` affect trust score directly? | No direct delta. Triggers a `NeedsCorroboration` warning + repair-job enqueue. If no corroboration found within 14 days, the claim downweights via `lack-of-corroboration` in `scoring::factors`, not via user-rejection. | §1 `CannotVerify`; §7 |
   114	| 3 | Does `New to me` need to exist as feedback? | No. It is a personal note/bookmark, not feedback. Use the existing notes affordance. | Out of scope |
   115	| 4 | Should user confidence be optional metadata? | No, hidden entirely for v1.4.0. A confidence slider is what the 5-point scale tried to be; rejected. May revisit in v1.5.0+ if a specific use case emerges. | Out of scope |
   116	| 5 | How many feedback actions can appear inline? | Six inline (the first six in §1) — short enough to not crowd a claim row, long enough to cover ~95% of expected user intent. The remaining three live in an overflow menu in v1.4.2. | Decided; UI implementation split per #1 |
   117	| 6 | Which claim types are editable directly vs. structured-only? | `NeedsNuance` with free text is allowed for: account assertions, person role descriptions, project status text. Structured-only (no `NeedsNuance`; only typed corrections via `WrongSubject` / `WrongSource` / `MarkFalse`): stakeholder roles, commitment owners, renewal dates, health bands. The structured-only set is anything where the UI already provides a typed input — text edit there would defeat the typing. | Decided; enforced by `claim_type` metadata on the claim row (DOS-7) |
   118	| 7 | Query/concurrency budget for targeted claim repair? | Per `CannotVerify`: one repair job enqueued, executes within 24 hours, capped at 1 LLM call + 1 retrieval batch (≤10 sources). Per entity: max 5 active repair jobs. Per workspace: max 50 active repair jobs. Excess queues with low priority. | §7; enforced via DOS-236 invalidation queue |
   119	
   120	### 4. Trust Compiler integration
   121	
   122	The Trust Compiler (DOS-5) consumes `ClaimFeedback` via the `user_feedback_weight` factor (per ADR-0114 R1.3, the factor takes pre-computed `age_days`, not a clock). Per-action deltas:
   123	
   124	| Action | Source weight Δ | Agent ledger Δ | Claim state | Freshness Δ | Subject evidence Δ |
   125	|---|---|---|---|---|---|
   150	- `corrected_to: None` — "this content does not belong to this entity; I don't know whose it is." Tombstone the claim at the asserted subject only; do not propose a new attachment.
   151	- `corrected_to: Some(SubjectRef)` — "this content belongs to that entity instead." Tombstone at the asserted subject; propose (not commit) a new claim at the corrected subject with the same claim_text, full provenance preserved, `actor = 'user_correction'`. The corrected claim enters via the standard propose/commit path so it gets the same gate as any other claim.
   152	
   153	The inference method that produced the wrong attachment receives `subject_evidence` downweight `-0.3`. After repeated `WrongSubject` feedback against the same `InferenceMethod` for the same source class, the inference method gets globally suppressed for that source class (threshold: 5 instances within 30 days).
   154	
   155	### 7. Repair scheduling for `CannotVerify`
   156	
   157	When `CannotVerify` is recorded:
   158	
   159	1. A `claim_repair_job` row is enqueued via DOS-236 invalidation queue with `priority = normal`.
   160	2. Job retrieves up to 10 candidate corroborating sources via Glean + local context.
   161	3. Job invokes a `find_corroborating_evidence` Read ability (lands in v1.4.1; v1.4.0 ships the queue + skeleton).
   162	4. Found corroboration → claim corroboration record (ADR-0113 R1.6); user notified.
   163	5. No corroboration within 14 days → claim downweights via `lack-of-corroboration` factor; lint surface flags it.
   164	
   165	Budgets: 1 LLM call + 1 retrieval batch per job; max 5 active repair jobs per entity; max 50 per workspace. Excess queues at `priority = low`.
   166	
   167	### 8. Non-goals for v1.4.0
   168	
   169	- UI surfaces beyond DOS-8's inline six. Overflow menu (`NeedsNuance` editor, `SurfaceInappropriate`, `NotRelevantHere`), claim detail panel, lint feedback debt — v1.4.2.
   170	- Cross-claim feedback aggregation ("user often marks Glean Salesforce sources wrong" → systematic source downweight). Tracked separately for v1.5.0+.
   171	- Feedback from non-User actors. Enum supports `created_by: ClaimActor` for future agent-to-agent feedback; v1.4.0 commits user-only.
   172	- Per-action delta tuning. The §4 numbers are starting values; v1.4.1 shadow data tunes them.
   173	- `find_corroborating_evidence` Read ability — v1.4.1; v1.4.0 ships the queue + skeleton.
   174	
   175	## Consequences
   176	
   177	### Positive
   178	
   179	- Trust Compiler's `user_feedback_weight` factor has a typed input, not a placeholder.
   180	- Per-action behavior is specified before code lands. No ambiguity about whether `MarkFalse` punishes the source or the claim.
   181	- `WrongSubject` becomes a substrate-level operation pairing cleanly with ADR-0105 `SubjectAttribution`. Subject mistakes do not punish sources.
   182	- `CannotVerify` does not punish claims for user uncertainty — closes the feedback-as-noise risk that yes/no would have introduced.
   183	- The 7 open decisions are answered before kickoff. No spec churn during the spine push.
   184	
   185	### Negative / risks
   186	
   187	- Nine variants is more than the placeholder three. UI overflow menu in v1.4.2 must handle the additional three cleanly.
   188	- `NeedsNuance` text-overlap heuristic for "refinement vs. contradiction" is approximate. Acceptable in v1.4.0; can be replaced with a small classifier in v1.4.1.
   189	- Per-action deltas in §4 are unvalidated starting values. Mitigated by shadow data + tuning in v1.4.1; the underlying tables are designed to recompute, so retroactive tuning is safe.
   190	- Repair job queue depends on DOS-236 (durable invalidation), which is v1.4.1 scope. v1.4.0 ships the `claim_repair_job` row schema + enqueue stub; the worker runs in v1.4.1. `CannotVerify` feedback collected in v1.4.0 is queued and processed once the worker lands.
   191	
   192	### Neutral
   193	
   194	- No new tables beyond `claim_feedback` and `claim_repair_job` (skeleton). ADR-0113's existing tables absorb the structural updates.
   195	- DOS-8 inline buttons remain; their handlers route to `propose_feedback(action)` rather than untyped boolean handlers.

